JF Mezei wrote:
> 
> 
> Does anyone know the real employee count at Compaq now ? And with Compaq's
> sales, what should this count be to have competitive productivity levels with
> the likes of HP, SUN and IBM ?

A quick browse of latest reported annual results (FY98):

	Empl.	Rev	Profit	Rev/Empl	Prof/Empl	% Prof Margin
HWP	124.6K	47B	3.21B	378K		25.7K		6.8
SUNW	26.3K	11.7	0.91B	446K		34.6K		7.8
IBM	291K	87.4B	6.76B	300K		23.2K		7.7
DELL	24.4K	19.9B	1.59B	815K		65.2K		8.0
CPQ	71.0K	38.5B  -2.48B	542K	       -34.9K	       -6.4

Doesn't quite answer your question but shows why CPQ must restructure. 
By adding Tandem & DEC, it went from a business model & cost structure
similar to SUNW & DELL to one closer to HWP & IBM.  Except HWP & IBM
have had decades learning how to be profitable with that structure/model
and CPQ's had only 6 qtrs.  Remember that IBM had several unprofitable
yrs before Gerstner.

CPQ's results include 1 pre-DEC qtr and it's likely that the company has
by now  already let go the 17K employees that were planned (although, as
you found out, they say nothing about current employement levels in the
latest earnings announcement).  So if they let go an additional 8K and
revenues stay flat, then its FY00 results would look like:

	Empl.	Rev	Profit	Rev/Empl	
CPQ	46.0K	37.6B	???	817K
				^^^^
This gives a clue to where Capellas is headed (DELL-like cost structure)
but also raises questions on what business model CPQ will settle on
(hint: DELL does not have DEC's "vaunted" sales & field service
overhead.  Nor does it have proprietory OSes, CPUs, platforms, channel
infrastructure/overhead, legacy customers & apps, associated R&D, etc). 
If CPQ attains this cost structure while maintaining an HWP or IBM-like
business model, then it will probably be praised as the new
DELL/M$/INTEL and case studies will be written about it.

One thing's for sure:  CPQ has reached one of its Pfeiffer-era corporate
objectives (to be the second largest computer company; HWP's results
includes 7.6B Test & Measurements business) even if it's unlikely to
achieve the other ($50B Revenues by Y2K).  Of course investors prefer
profit margins and earnings-per-share as measures of mgmt effectiveness.

Bill



