> }Dave
> 
> Are you sure that it did?
> 
> There seems to me to be 3 ways to interpret the statement:
> 
> "We will A, and we will B, and we will C as long as it is profitable"
> can be either
> 
>   (A) and (B) and (C as long as it is profitable)
> 
> or it can be
> 
>   (A) and ((B and C) as long as it is profitable)
> 
>   (this goes with the division between "support" and "promote")
> 
> or it can be
> 
>   (A and B and C) as long as it is profitable
> 
> It's impossible to say which was meant without hearing it, and even
> those who heard it probably can't really be certain which was meant.
> 
> I suspect that the "we will keep doing things that are profitable and
> stop doing things that are not profitable" is the best interpretation
> of what was meant.
> 
> --- Carl

Yeah, as I read this post I thought that it's possible that the statements
could possibly have been taken out of context.  Many things are possible.
However, the longer I stared at the screen, the more I felt that there are
problems that are not being fixed.

Tell ya a story.  Frank and Dave started a company to provide technical
services.  Company grows and is doing well.  Most of the business is in
the VMS environment, and the majority of the personnel are long time VMS
people.  The general consensus among most of the people is that if we don't
drop the VMS work and get into NT work, we won't have a future.  Dave's
the lone VMS advocate, and the rest think he's a bit nuts.  One very
capable person even left the company because it wasn't dropping VMS quick
enough.  He didn't like the direction.  Note that the majority of revenues
are still being produced by VMS work!  I can only ask, "how can such as
attitude prevail when it is counter to what's very successful?"  The answer
is very easy, one word, PRECEPTION!  DEC actively pushed VMS customers toward
NT!  Compaq is still promoting NT for jobs where VMS would work much better!
In looking at this, maybe Dave is the nut case, and the rest are right!

If Compaq doesn't want VMS customers to get the preception that they should
begin planning their migration off VMS, they have to do more than SUPPORT
VMS, they have to "P R O M O T E" VMS.  When a small customer who wants to
get into e-business (or whatever) comes to them they should say "We can start
you with a DS10, and forget about NT or Linux, use VMS at the same cost as NT,
and when you get bigger, you can add larger systems to a cluster without
any changes to your application or business disruption.  Why, if you get big
enough and see the need, you can have multiple sites at widely spaced
geographical locations for redundancy and disaster tolerance without any
changes to your application or business disruption."  When promotion like this
happens, then customers will know that Compaq believes in VMS, and the
customers can feel comfortable with planning their future around VMS.

So, any statement by Compaq's CEO which contains a reference to VMS, and you
have to hope you heard something that wasn't negative concerning VMS, just
doesn't cut it.  If it wasn't clearly and very distinctly a promotion of VMS
then the trade rags are going to hear and report something negative and based
upon the past who can blame them.

I hope for the best, but must ask, if Compaq truly believes in VMS, why can't
we hear the word PROMOTE just a few times?  It won't hurt them to use that
word, but they don't.  Even if they support new development, they have a
perception problem, and the more they refuse to promote VMS, the longer
that problem will last, and it will grow.

(Now falling off soapbox.)

Dave

