Arnaud ABRASSART <noar@styx.obspm.fr> wrote in message
news:379ECB5F.AE2C23B@styx.obspm.fr...
>   I didn t get any doc with the laptop
> how do I find out if there is a protected boot block ?
> is this jumper enabled ?
> what if there are none ?
> what kind of work is ahead of me ?
> is it nearly impossible ?
> could you point me to any source that describe the way to go in that
case ?
>
Sorry, I don't know anything about your particular machine.  If the boot
ROM is completely erased, essentially you have a computer that cannot be
booted.  You would have to find a source for the original BIOS, then
remove the flash IC, program it in a device programmer, and install it
back in the laptop.

Most, but not all, PC designs have a portion of the flash ROM that
cannot be erased, so even in the worst case you always have a minimal
program to start up and recover.  Unfortunately, protected boot blocks
in flash are a fairly recent development, older designs do not have this
feature and are vulnerable to an unrecoverable crash if the flash
programming is interrupted.  In that case you have to externally program
the flash again, removing it from the circuit board, putting it in a
flash/EPROM programmer, loading a new copy of the BIOS (no idea where
you might get it), then putting the chip back in the laptop.  If the
flash ROM is soldered in you have to unsolder it, a task not for the
faint of heart.  If it's surface mounted, you will need special
machinery to remove and reinstall it.

Did you get a real good deal on this laptop?  Most likely its because
the BIOS is trashed and it's too expensive to get it fixed.
   Jack Peacock




