Herewith a missive that crossed my transom a couple of days ago.


From:	President & CEO - Michael D. Capellas
Sent:	Friday, July 23, 1999 
Subject:	Let's build Compaq's future together
To: Compaq Worldwide Team

Today is my first full day as CEO of Compaq, and I couldn't be more
excited to have this job . . . at this company . . . at this moment in
the history of the information technology industry.  I'm a relative
newcomer to Compaq; I joined the company last August.  But I've been
here long enough to appreciate the company's tremendous strengths - its
technology, its customers, its partners and, most important, you.  It is
a privilege to lead the best team in the industry.   

What makes this particularly exciting is that we are at the dawn of a
new era in information technology.  I've spent my entire career in IT,
and I have never seen such dramatic changes.  The Internet is
transforming the way companies do business and the way they interact
with their customers, partners and suppliers.   And it is clear to me
that business needs are moving toward Compaq's strengths.

To realize the promise of eBusiness, companies need the products,
services and solutions that we deliver better than anyone else in the
industry.  This includes next generation networks . . .
high-performance, high-availability servers . . . networked storage . .
. Internet PCs . . . and appliances and devices that enable companies
and individuals to connect to the Internet in new and more productive
ways.

Our objective is to maximize the benefits of eBusiness for our customers
- from consumers to global enterprises.  We will do that in several
ways.  We will capitalize on high-availability technologies to drive
NonStop eBusiness Solutions.  We will continue to drive industry
standards.  We will capture new categories of users.  We will integrate
services into every offering, adding significant value to our products
and solutions.  And we will lower the cost and risk of deploying
eBusiness. 

Compaq is already doing these things today for stock exchanges, banks
and telecommunications companies, and for Internet leaders like
Amazon.com, Microsoft Network, Priceline.com, Drugstore.com, AltaVista,
Lycos and many others.

I'm sure you have seen articles suggesting that Compaq is late to the
Internet.  It is true that we have not moved at Internet speed.  But
eBusiness is still in its early stages.  The technology is evolving, and
in many cases it is still immature.  For every Amazon.com or Schwab,
there are scores of companies that are still struggling with their
eBusiness strategies.  They are now beginning to recognize that
eBusiness success means constant access, all the time, nonstop.  The
opportunity for Compaq is enormous - if we execute.

Execution really is the key.  To improve execution and to drive
profitable growth, we will focus on five key priorities.  

� Establish global business groups.  As we announced in June, we are
realigning around three global business groups - Enterprise Solutions
and Services, Commercial Personal Computing and Consumer.  Each business
group is responsible for its strategy, for rationalizing its portfolio
consistent with Internet leadership, for building a competitive model
and for speeding time to market.  Our goal is for each of you to wake up
in the morning knowing who your customers are and who you're competing
against.

� Complete the management team.  We are well on our way.  The operations
management team is in place.  We expect to fill the remaining vacancies
during the new few weeks.  I am pleased that we have been able to form a
very cohesive team in a short time.

� Accelerate innovation.  The Internet is all about innovation and
speed, and we must be in the forefront of bringing new products,
services and solutions to market more quickly than our competitors.

� Simplify distribution.  No matter how we go to market - directly or
through resellers - we must be efficient and responsive to our
customers.  Building a world-class supply chain and continuing to
improve our customer choice model are essential.

� Communicate clearly and credibly.  We need to do a better job of
communicating our strengths.  But we must also be as open and honest as
possible in discussing our challenges and how we are dealing with
them.     
    
I have already begun small group sessions with employees to talk about
the company and where we are headed.  I will hold these meetings around
the world.  I want to meet as many of you as I can during the next few
months and hear your thoughts about how we can succeed together.

This is what you can expect of me.  I will be passionate and involved. 
I will communicate openly, honestly and often.  I will never sacrifice
the future.  And I will do what I say.  I expect you to be an advocate
for the customer . . . to build cool stuff . . . to do what you say . .
. and to have some fun. 

Today we are putting the competition on notice: together, we're taking
the fight to the marketplace.  And we will win.  This company has
transformed the information technology industry before, and we will do
it again.  We recognize our challenges, and we will address them with a
renewed sense of pride, commitment and confidence.   We have a great
future.  It started today.

Michael



