"David J. Dachtera" wrote:
> 
> > "Terry C. Shannon" wrote:
> [snip]
> > > As for OpenVMS, it remains quite profitable (gross margin was 58 percent
> > > a year ago)... [snip]
> 
> Now: Here's a point that I'd like some help trying to communicate to
> "the appropriate people at Compaq".
> 
> Which would you rather have:
> 
> 58% of $50 million ($29 million) or 29% of $150 million ($43.5 million)?
> 
> (Flame me not - I realize that halving the margin on OpenVMS does not
> guarantee a sustainable 3-fold increase in sales ...but what if I'm
> right? ...and what if my estimate of 3-fold is way low?)
> 
> Does *THIS* begin to clarify the question of whether or not Affordable
> OpenVMS remains profitable?
> 
> --
> David J. Dachtera
> dba DJE Systems
> http://home.earthlink.net/~djesys/
> 
> Unofficial Affordable OpenVMS Home Page and Message Board:
> http://home.earthlink.net/~djesys/vms/soho/

Well, I agree with you in principal, but question the changes.  Making VMS
affordable at the low end does not necessarily mean halfing the gross margin.
Then there's the question of how the gross margin is calculated, and remember
that net margin is more of something you can take to the bank.

If e-business is one of your goals, then pitch (PROMOTE) a low cost system
designed to host web pages and go after the ISPs.  Tell them that they can
start with very low costs, and when they become successful, it will cost more
money, but they can just plug in more capability rather than having to re-design
their operations to handle the increase in business.  Just 1 idea.

As said many times, "It's the marketing!"

Dave

