JF Mezei wrote:
> 
> Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> > Quite possibly the denizens of comp.os.vms were not the intended
> > audience of this business-oriented statement.  The section about
> > "so long as it is profitable" might be a sideways method of saying
> > to investors:
> 
> Sorry, but the "dropping/realigning products" bit is becoming just as
> incredible (lack of credibility) as the musical chair re-orgs that Palmer
> would announce following any bad financial news.

Scott McNealy summed it up nicely during his January 1999 annual analyst
briefing. The theme of Scott's Top Ten List was "the Top Ten Reasons You
Know Bill Clinton is on your Board of Directors."

Reason Number One? Airstrikes precede bad earnings announcements.

Substitute "reorg" for "airstrike" and the statement is apropos of DEC.

> 
> Palmer dropped unprofitable products before Compaq bought it. Then during the
> merger, Compaq took a hard look at DEC's product suite and dropped overlapping
> stuff (eg: PCs, Laptops etc).

Not to mention DEC Wintel servers. But none of this was unexpected.

> 
> Now, some newbie at Compaq who get the top job is going to drop unprofitable
> products ? Because he announced this, he *will* have to drop some products
> just to show Wall Street that he is able to weild an axe. (even if such
> products were profitable or generated sales for profitable products).
> 
> Also, and most importantly, Compaq acquired a whole bunch of stuff whose
> potential was never realised due to DEC's inability to market them. And a
> short term wall-street-pleasing dropping of products may in fact kill off
> potential treasures that could had developped had Compaq started to admit to
> their existence.

I have no idea what products Capellas will choose to drop, and I agree
that the "at long as it's profitable" suffix on the OpenVMS statement
was not a politically correct (or prudent) thing to say.

Still, I find the downsizing issue to be more interesting. Wall Street
beancounters don't know VMS from ALL-IN-1, but they do know that layoffs
are a Good Thing. And based on what Mike said on June 17, incremental
layoffs seem downright inevitable.

Again, from the July 23 Shannon Knows Compaq...

"Rationalization" and "reduction" go hand-in-hand, so it wasn�t
surprising that Capellas was queried on the "L-word." The CEO said
Compaq last year planned a total workforce reduction of 17K. "We've
taken 15K, and we'll take out the rest.� SKC believes the cuts will be
significantly deeper: Compaq's November 1998 10-Q report cited an
aggregate headcount reduction of 19.7K. And Capellas on June 17 said
Compaq will continue to eliminate layers of  redundancy. "We�ll have
some element of [work] force reduction" in the future, said the
then-COO. More details should become known when Compaq on August 15
rolls out its rationalization plan. 

> 
> There is a lot of marketing work needed to be done for almost all of DEC's
> former products before one can conclude that they are not profitable. I hope
> Capellas realises that CXompaq bought a very sick Digital and he shoudl
> perhaps try some medecine before amputating.

Capellas kicked off his press conference by expressing his hope that the
meeting represented "the start of an open communication process" with
the press and analyst community. One of the five agenda items in his
July 23 "Let's Build Compaq's Future Together" employee letter was
"communicate clearly and directly." I would hope that Mike has gotten
the message that "it's the marketing, stupid."

Those of us who covered Digital in the past found Compaq to be far less
capable in this regard. Maybe this will change.

Terry Shannon



