I agree.  These days you can slap Linux on just about any system.  Its tough
to compete with free software (even though they get you with support).  I
don't think senior management, the folks thay approve purchases, has any
appreciation for the nuts and bolts behind any operating system.  They just
hear buzz words.  Remember how "Open" Unix was supposed to be.  Now we have
at least a half dozen variants - and now Linux (unix by another name) is
going the same route.  After, 4 classes at Sun and 1 at HP, I'm still
scratching my head as to how Unix even got its foot in the door given how
much more robust VMS really is in comparison and that anyone who ever worked
on both knew it.  I know, I know - its the marketing.

The reason why Compaq can get a 58% profit margin is because they have us by
the short hairs.  VAXes/Alphas aren't called legacy systems for nothing.
Even folks who want to dump VMS can't do it overnight.  To add insult to
injury, we all know that VMS is only the beginning.  DEC was (and Compaq
still is) notorious for nickel and diming you for layered product licenses.
How many times has your company put off buying new hardware (listen up
Compaq) because you couldn't afford the increased units that LMF would
require for the same exact binaries on a higher end machine.

What I'm trying to say is that in the end NEW SALES are going to be defined
by the HARDWARE that a computer company offers.  Sun and HP can't touch
Compaq's (DEC's) engineering but Compaq needs to make their best operating
system, VMS, more affordable so that the aggregate sale of an Alpha with all
the fixings (os and layered products) is reasonable.

Just my 2 cents,
Mike Flaherty
