In article <3792ECA9.B516F73B@uk.sun.com>, Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy <andrew.nospam@uk.sun.com> writes:

> True, you did include that in your scenario. The suprise
> wasn't that Digital thought that there was a SMP
> scalability limit but that at 6-8 CPU's it was so very
> pessimistic.

Really ?

So what is the _worst_ scalability that can be achieved using a
Sun machine ?  Take an application coded to encounter every
bottleneck possible in Solaris.

Now put that application in the hands of a customer who wants to
scale better without redesigning the application.  How can you
help them from the operating system level?

There is no question that it is possible to write _some_ applications
that scale well in SMP -- the fact that anyone ever built a second
MPP machine proves that.  The question is how well a _typical_
application will do.

Obviously operating system features cannot cover all application
design errors -- a single global "transaction in progress" lock
by the application is a trivial counterexample.  But where the
application performance problems involve operating system usage,
help may be possible.

The shape of the bell curve (or non-bell curve) regarding how
many applications are designed to scale well vs. poorly is up
for debate.  Qualifying that by "important" applications just
opens up the debate regarding which applications are "important".
I know you have your pet list of financial applications, Andrew,
but for VMS the "important" applications include use in military
aircraft as reported recently by Jeff Killeen on DECUServe (only
Jeff actually remembers model numbers of airplanes).  These may
be applications that have not been noticed by Sun UK, like the
database behind the Northern Lights search engine.

Larry Kilgallen



