1-1.5 CPU's but thats a worse case. Something like Firewall-1for instance.

This isn't due to any I/O bottlenecks but simply because the
Firewall-1 inspection module is single threaded and you can
only run one module.

Other applications like Notes used to scale to 2 CPU's but
have been re-engineered to scale to many more CPU's.

But this isn't necessarely typical even for firewalls, Sun's EFS
product is threaded and scales to many more CPU's.

So what you get are applications that are designed to scale,
Oracle, Sybase, Informix, EFS etc. Applications like Notes which
didn't scale but now do because they have been re-engineered
and applications that don't scale at all.

What we are seing is increasing numbers of applications that
have been threaded, or designed to use some sort of mechanism
to distribute processing accross CPU's.

> Now put that application in the hands of a customer who wants to
> scale better without redesigning the application.  How can you
> help them from the operating system level?
>

I am not clear how Galaxy will help this. Either the applicationscales,
like Oracle or Sybase or EFS or Notes etc or it does
not. What does Galaxy buy me if the app isn't designed to scale
and if the application is designed to scale allready, like Oracle
or Sybase why do I need Galaxies ?

Do I need as a developer foe example to re-develop my
application to make use of Galaxy features ?

Now I accept that current SMP machines probably top out at
64 CPU's though Sequent are talking about 256 CPU SMP
systems but this seems to squeeze Galaxies into the Ultra
high end, assuming that they work as advertised.

> There is no question that it is possible to write _some_ applications
> that scale well in SMP -- the fact that anyone ever built a second
> MPP machine proves that.  The question is how well a _typical_
> application will do.

I am not sure about your MPP point but the portfolio of appsthat scale well on SMP machines is quite large if you
include
applications build on Oracle, Sybase or Informix.

Perhaps the illustation of this is Sun's sucess with the E10K.
People tend to buy machines to run apps and there are a lot of large

>32 CPU E10K's out there.

