>>
> 
> I am not clear how Galaxy will help this. Either the applicationscales,
> like Oracle or Sybase or EFS or Notes etc or it does
> not. What does Galaxy buy me if the app isn't designed to scale
> and if the application is designed to scale allready, like Oracle
> or Sybase why do I need Galaxies ?

I was talking about an application which scales poorly, and again you
have attempted to change the subject to an application which already
scales well.  Once and for all, let me concede the point -- applications
that scale well do scale well.  Satisfied ?

Now as to applications that do not scale well (which is what we were
discussing before you tried to change the subject)...  one matter in
which an application can run into a scaling barrier is in the manner
it uses system resources.  If the application forces locking contention
inside the OS above a certain SMP count, making the OS contention
regions (for things like kernel pool, etc.) orthogonal to application
existence can reduce that contention.

> Do I need as a developer foe example to re-develop my
> application to make use of Galaxy features ?

No, you need a system manager to set up the instances and say which
applications will use which instances.

> I am not sure about your MPP point but the portfolio of apps
> that scale well on SMP machines is quite large if you
> include
> applications build on Oracle, Sybase or Informix.

It certainly seems to me that that only covers database applications.
Yes, database vendors work hard to make their part scale well, as it
provides a multiplier effect.  Historically there have been  various
pieces of VMS that seem to have been added _only_ for the support
of database applications.

Larry Kilgallen



