Hi. I'm new to the UNIX admin game and I have what I hope is a
straightforward question:

This is for an Alpha Server 2100 running DU 3.2c

Our rz0 disk is 1.2 gig in size and nearing capacity. We have a 4 gig
drive that we would like to replace it  with. At the same time we would
like to resize / from 64 to 128 mb and /usr from 1.7 to 2.5 gig.

What would be the least painful way to do this?

Possibilities:

- vdump from current rz0. Physically pull it out of the machine.
disklabel the new drive as rz0 and partition it in the sizes we want.
vrestore the directories into their new home.

- vdump from current rz0. disklabel rz0 something else (doesn't matter
what). disklabel the new drive as rz0 and partition it in the sizes we
want. vrestore the directories into their new home.

Do either or neither of these make sense?? Is there a better way? Again,
I'm new at this so be gentle!

thanks,

david.

ps. this is also in anticipation of upgrading from 3.2c to 4.0e




