Didier Donner wrote:
> 
> Hello,
> 
> I have just been to Compaq's web site for the XP1000 workstation
> and it found that
> * a XP 900 is listed
> * a 667 Mhz processor and a 21264A processor are mentionned for the XP1000
> 
> What are the characteristics of the 21264A ? is it a 667 Mhz 21264 ?
> On the Alpha Processor Inc's website, I found reference of the 750 Mhz
> 21264.
> Can someone help me figure out this frequency dance ?

   The "A" suffix to indicate a slight shrink and/or higher
   speed grade of a processor goes back as far as the "high
   speed" 4.0 MHz Z-80A   ;-)  MIPS also used it for a while
   with the R3000 -> R3000A but then decided to bump the
   numbers instead (R4000 -> R4400).  Maybe they thought it
   looked like they were doing more development that way.

   In the case of Alpha its use has been sporadic.  The first
   generation part the 21064 was built in 0.75 um and ran as
   fast as 200 MHz (although yields seemed to be centered
   closer to 166 MHz or so).  It was tweaked with a 10% shrink
   to 0.68 um but the designation didn't change. The 21064A
   version was both a shrink to 0.50 um and the on-chip caches
   were doubled in size.  It ran as fast as 300 MHz although
   266 and 275 MHz were more popular.

   The second generation part initially kept up this notation
   with the 0.5 um 21164 and 0.35 um 21164A.  Then for some
   inexplicable reason the A was dropped and all 2nd gen parts
   were called 21164 regardless of process.  (Maybe DEC semi
   tired of confusion with Canadian customers - "We would would
   like to buy 5000 21164s, eh? :)   BTW, the 0.35 um part was
   modified slightly to add 8 and 16 bit load and store
   instructions.

   The third generation part 21264 was initially built in the
   0.35 um CMOS.  The shrink to 0.28 um has recently been
   termed the 21264A so maybe DEQ is going back to the "A"
   notation.  Perhaps when 0.18 um 21264s are available these
   will be denoted as the 21264B (just like the 6 MHz Z-80B)
   The 0.35 um 21264 was advertised as going fast as 600 MHz
   although the fastest shipped in a system is the low volume
   575 MHz upgrade card to the 8400.  Considering the Vdd and
   power increase that got put into the final 21264 datasheet
   I suspect the 0.35 um part doesn't yield well much above
   500 MHz.  So far the 21164A has been announced in 667 and
   750 MHz grades but I predict it may eventually support a
   third, higher speed bin (833 MHz ?) before it is EOL'd.

   The interesting question is what minor changes were introduced
   when the 21264 was ported to 0.28 um? Engineers love to tweak
   stuff. If there were no changes in the port then it would
   be a first for Alpha and I would be sorely disappointed.  I
   doubt the L1 caches were increased in size, we would have
   heard about it; it would also be dumb because first priority
   with the 21264 is to reduce die size from the current 314 mm2.
   My guess is that minor tweaks to hardwired heuristics and the
   branch prediction schemes have been made to incorporate the
   lessons learned from studying traces of real apps.  We might
   learn more about this at the Hot Chips conference next month.

> 
> Best regards,
> 
> Didier Donner


All opinions strictly my own.
