Darin,

Ya got yourself a nice box there.

If you run a dual-processor setup, make sure that the CPUs have
matched steppings.  The BSOD implies unmatched steppings, but it could
have been caused by something else.  Could also be a bad cache module,
which (BTW) is proprietary, despite having the same physical layout as
the common industry-standard COAST modules.

DEC used three generations of processor cards in your beast.  All the
processor cards look the same, have the same chip components, but have
different sets of jumpers.  54-24070 should be the "second" generation
by my recollection.  

The 54-24000-01 card is common to all three processor cards, and is
required for any Pentium up thru 133MHz.  Another module or card is
required for faster Pentium CPUs, supported only by the last Celebris
XL processor card, 54-24490.  You need only one 54-24000-01 to run
dual-processor.

Jumpers and their settings are somewhat in common among the three
processor cards:

J2 1-2=66MHz, 2-3=60MHz
J4 ON 2/1 bus multiplier, OFF 3/2 bus multiplier
J8 ON pipeline burst-mode cache, OFF non-pipelined

The correct jumper settings for 133Mhz are J2 1-2, J4 ON.

On the suspicion that the cache is not working right:

1. Disable external cache in system CMOS setup.
2. Or remove the cache module.
3. Change the J8 jumper setting.

Update the flash BIOS to the latest from the DEC website.  Make sure
you use the right one.  The version is 2.04, and it is Y2K compliant.

We're one of the last storehouses of parts on the North American
continent for these computers.  If you need some parts, let us know...

