>
>  Computer Arhitecture and Parallel Processing, Kai Hwang and
>  Faye A. Briggs, McGraw Hill, 1984.
>
>  In 1.3.4, second paragraph "The lower-bound log2n is known as
>  the Minsky's conjecture.  Most commercial multiprocessor systems
>  have from n = 2 rto n - 4 processors.  Exploratory research
>  multiprocessors have challenged n = 16 processors in C.mmp and
>  S-1 systems.  Using Minsky's conjecture, only a speedup of
>  2 to 4 can be expected from existing multiprocessors with 4
>  to 16 processors.  This sounds rather pessimistic.  A more
>  optimistic speedup estimate is upper bounded by n/ln n as
>  derived below."

This might be referring to "On Some Associative, Parallel and Analog
Computations," (with S. Papert), @i[Associative Information Techniques,] E.L.
Jacks, ed., American Elsevier Publishing, Inc., 1971, pp.  27-47. 

Umm, as I recall it, this simple idea was based on just looking at conditional
branches.  Suppose a computer goes a mean of K steps before hitting a
conditional branch (that is, one that depends on what has been computed in that
interval).  Then, in any straightforward parallel speedup scheme, if you're
trying to 'look-ahead' past that branch, you need two processors to start
anticipating those two branches.  (You also need to 'guess' how to initialize
those processes--but let's assume optimistically that you don't need to change
any parameters.  Not likely, of course.) 

Then, if you try to go even faster, you must double the number of processors
every K units of lookahead time, hence the gloomy order of logN speedup for N
processors.

Of course, this assumes that there's no smarter way to compute the thing, e.g.,
by using huge tables, etc.  But then we gave an informal argument, based on
Shannon's proof that most circuits cannot be simplified (that is, rewritten as
composite functions of simpler functions)--hence, in general, there do not
exist 'smarter' ways to rewrite arbitrary programs.  However, such a conclusion
would not generally apply to "interesting" processes with meaningful
structures, so it would be hard to formally prove any such thing about actual
programs.

(One might even try to use a Chaitin-like complexity argument to prove that it
can't be proved.  Anyone out there good at translating Chaitin's methods into
..




