  Configurations with thousands of CPUs are not particularly unusual.

:> There is no question that it is possible to write _some_ applications
:> that scale well in SMP -- the fact that anyone ever built a second
:> MPP machine proves that.  The question is how well a _typical_
:> application will do.
:
:I am not sure about your MPP point but the portfolio of appsthat scale 
:well on SMP machines is quite large if you include
:applications build on Oracle, Sybase or Informix.

  The underlying database can certainly be a factor, but it is certainly 
  not necessarily the limiting factor -- the bottleneck -- in application 
  SMP scaling.

:Perhaps the illustation of this is Sun's sucess with the E10K.
:People tend to buy machines to run apps and there are a lot of large
:32 CPU E10K's out there.

  SMP is one answer to improving system performance -- like most any other 
  factor in computer system design, it is a trade-off.

  Older (as it was then known) VAX/VMS systems had better SMP scaling than 
  the contemporary Sun systems, but the Sun systems had better uniprocessor 
  performance.  Since then, Sun has emphasized higher SMP scaling factors, 
  while OpenVMS Alpha emphasizes uniprocessor performance.

