In article <sfqr9m42zkf.fsf@kraftwerk.pa.dec.com>,
Aaron Spink  <spink@kraftwerk.pa.dec.com> wrote:
>lindahl@pbm.com (Greg Lindahl) writes:
>
>> Your limit is N=64. Your competition is building SMP machines several
>> times larger, and shared-less machines with simpler ideas of a single
>> system image are several times larger than that.
>> 
>And there is at least a decent amount of evidence that they don't
>scale to 64 nodes without going into a different programming model
>that is not the classical smp model.  

Maybe....  We certainly have proof that some non-trivial codes
exist that scale to 256p while retaining an SMP programming model.
These do *not* parallelize each loop independently -- the overheads
would kill unless you had enormous/impractical problem sizes.
Instead, the parallelization is on a larger scale.  Sometimes this
requires significant code modification and sometimes it does not.

Most of the codes that appear to scale beyond 64p are in computational
fluid dynamics (including weather and ocean modelling), bioinformatics/
gene sequencing, computational chemistry, and some cryptography codes.


>> You're saying this without being aware of the companies who are
>> shipping such products?
>> 
>Only 1.  SGI will ship 64,128, and now 256 cpu machines.  Maybe we can
>get McCalpin to describe the types of workloads being run on those
>machines?  I seriously doubt they are the same programs that run on 4
>and 16 way cpu Origins.

They are not typically the same programs, but this is more a resource
allocation issue than anything else.  The 256p machines are reserved 
for very large jobs that scale very well -- smaller jobs that scale
poorly will naturally be shifted to the smaller systems.


>There is research out there that validates that big SMPs can scale,
>but the programming model is vastly different than what you use on a
>4way smp.  In a lot of ways it closer to a MPP of 2 cpu nodes with
>really fast interconnect.

It is certainly possible to run MPP-style message-passing codes on
large ccNUMA machines, but that is not required for performance.

There are at least three categories of codes:

(1) SMP codes that, by good fortune/foresight, are actually
    NUMA-friendly and scale very well with minimal modifications.

    For example, the MM5 weather code is already set up so that a
    single subroutine call will do all the processing for a single
    2D slice of the 3D domain.  Parallelizing the loop containing
    that subroutine call provides excellent speedups out to ~256p.
    A similar situation exists for "GASP", a high-speed aero-
    dynamics code.  Unlike the regular grid structure of MM5,
    GASP uses a multi-domain, block-structured mesh, and so is more
    representative of industrial applications.

(2) Codes that have to be considered a hybrid of SMP and message-
    passing techniques.

    An example is NASA's "OVERFLOW" code, another multi-domain
    block-structured code.  For this code, about 800 of the 500,000
    lines were changed to support a multi-host SYSTEM V shared
    memory arena to allow parallelization across multiple systems.
    Some "tweaks" were provided to accelerate the data transfer
    between systems -- these are analogous to explicit messages.

(3) Traditional MPI/PVM/SHMEM codes.

    An example is FLUENT/UNS, a computational fluid dynamics code
    using MPI messaging to get excellent parallel scaling on a 
    variety of machines.  Other example of MPI codes can be found
    at the NCSA web site.

As a rough estimates, I would say that a third of the codes running
on large Origin2000 systems are of each class.  (I include PPM and
its derivatives in the second class, just because so much work has
gone into the parallelization that it can hardly be considered an
"ordinary" SMP code.)




>I would also bet a dollar that the sgi's are being used and targeted
>toward a vastly different market than VMS bases systems are.

That seems like a safe bet.
