VMS that go far beyond limited Denial of Service exploits.

It is in general, much much more difficult to carefully
craft, say,  a buffer-overrun exploit under VMS, in such a way 
that one can trick the kernel into executing arbitrary code, 
in system/privileged context, on your behalf.  

this is due in part to mult-tiered memory page protections,
and in part, because VMS typically uses counted-strings
and string descriptors to pass arguments.    

This pedantic design is something that has to be built-in 
from ground up, and it has much to do with the 
seemingly arrogant 'assumptions'  made by us VMS bigots.

I've seen literally dozens of detailed recipes to do this 
sort of exploit under NT, and some flavors of UNIX 
