:Is there a definitive list somewhere of what (non DEC) SCSI CDROM drives
:will/will not work in a Vax/VMS system?

  Nope, not that I am aware of.

:I know they need to be able to cope with  512 bytes per sector, either by
:jumpering or via a SCSI mode command,
:but apart from, "this one works" "this one don't" anecdotes from users of
:same, I have yet to find a list.

  AFIAK, that level of anecdotal compatibility information is all that 
  is available.

:I have several
:SCSI CDROMS available to me, and I'm hoping at least one can be made to talk
:to my CMD CBI-1000 VAXBI SCSI Controller.
:I have looked in the VMS FAQ and found little that is useful on this
:subject.  Perhaps this could be added?

  The complexity of maintaining and updating such a device compatibility 
  list is not something that OpenVMS FAQ is really targeted nor is (IMO)
  really suitable for.  The folks here in Compaq StorageWorks and OpenVMS 
  Engineering have a sufficiently "entertaining" time maintaining the 
  existing SCSI product compatibility matrixes. 

  As you get deeper into SCSI, the more you learn about how perverse,
  fickle, or bizzare individual SCSI implementations can be, and the
  sorts of weird configuration problems that can (and do) creep in.
  (You really do have to make occasional sacrifices to ensure the 
  continued viability of your local SCSI chain. :-)

  To be generally useful, any compatibility chart would have to include 
  the host operating system version(s) tested, the host platform(s) tested,
  the device firmware version, the specific host controller, the specific
  SCSI storage controller (if applicable), the other widgets present on the 
  bus, and required device settings (if any), the specific device vendor,
  the console firmware version, and probably a few other items I have 
  forgotten.  This is a lot of information, and is not something easily 
  suited for inclusion into a document like the OpenVMS FAQ.

  In other words, what you get when you purchase an RRD-series SCSI CD-ROM 
  drive is the knowledge that the integration and testing work has been 
  performed for you, and that the device will work.

  I and others have discussed starting a hardware FAQ.

  There are presently various hardware-related websites listed in the FAQ.

  The above are my opinions and are intended to give you some idea of what 
  is involved in the request -- also realize I am not the keeper of the FAQ.

