Let me suggest this kind of "conspiracy theory" is off the
mark. UCX is a layered product. If someone asks VMS Engineering
whether VMS is affected by, say, the LAND attack, the answer
is no, and also the answer is that VMS Engineering hasn't got UCX
code, no access to it, and no way to answer. The UCX group isn't
even in the same state, and I had heard that UCX maintenance
has been shifted to Process Software for the older stacks anyhow.

CERT may ask "the vendors", but word very likely never got to
the right folks at DEC for some cases. (I have seen others where
definite answers did come out, so the system seems to have worked
sometimes.) When answers did come out they tended to cover several
of the TCP/IP stacks for VMS...even the free CMU one.

Assuming that UCX is part of the OS is a unix-centric view
(which is to be expected from a unix vendor, but not everywhere
the case). It tends to be licensed with new machines these
days, but that was not always so either, and other vendors still
sell their stacks.

I'm in fact somewhat amazed that similar issues don't arise
at Sun. Or do they?

