   The "est" SPECmarks are what the vendor feels he has in the bag
   (either from prelim hardware or from simulation) when the press
   release etc is written up and approved.  Wait until the official
   SPEC results are submitted before making final judgement.  These
   are often 5 - 10% better depending how conservative the original
   estimate was, how much tuning was done afterwards, and the level
   of mind games being played against competitors.

   Compare the SPECintbase95 numbers of the XP1000/500 to the
   API UP2000 (these are similar - 4 MB L2, 4 "D" chips):

       UP2000/667  31.8    (running NT !!!)
       XP1000/500  23.2

                   ratio is 1.37 instead of 1.33

   Now factor in a constant level of memory performance (I believe
   the XP1000/500, XP1000/667, and UP2000 all run their system bus
   at 333 MHz) and the fact that the UP2000 was running Bill's swill
   and this ratio should be even larger under identical circumstances.
   There may be some L2 timing differences but the available info
   isn't detailed enough to nail this factor down.

   Does anyone have any examples of Alpha SPECmarks under NT and
   TRU64 for identical hardware? The deltas would be enlightening.

[snip]

