> >> With this change, this code scales extremely well, delivering
> >> something along the lines of a >200x speedup on a 256p system.
> >
> >Can you give a citation for this? 
> 
> http://www.ncsa.uiuc.edu/SCD/Conferences/Origin2000/Presentations/Conf.Jones.ppt

This result is for a very large problem size. At a smaller size (70x
less data), John Michalakes found that the distributed memory version
of mm5 (i.e. think message passing on an SGI) runs significantly
faster than the OpenMP version. I extended his result when I showed
that a cluster of Alphas with Myrinet were significantly faster than
an SGI and an IBM SP-2 on this code and test case.

mm5 is the kind of code where larger is easier. I believe that most
users are running at the small size, but I'm hardly an expert about
the userbase. The big problem size is probably the cause of your nice
scaling to large numbers of processors.

John Michalakes talk can be found at:

http://www-unix.mcs.anl.gov/~michalak/daoslides/

The interesting slide is:

http://www-unix.mcs.anl.gov/~michalak/daoslides/sld037.htm

which shows the "DM" memory model scaling much better than the "SM"
memory model.

In short: if you're going to claim that a simple shared memory model
is good enough, you need to temper that claim by considering the
problem size, and the scaling (and coding cost) of alternatives.  The
usual claim is that a "fine grain" machine like the SGI is better for
demanding small problems, but in this case you're throwing that
advantage away with the OpenMP programming model.

-- g



