>> >> With this change, this code scales extremely well, delivering
>> >> something along the lines of a >200x speedup on a 256p system.
>> >
>> >Can you give a citation for this? 
>> 
>> http://www.ncsa.uiuc.edu/SCD/Conferences/Origin2000/Presentations/Conf.Jones.ppt
>
>This result is for a very large problem size. 
>
>In short: if you're going to claim that a simple shared memory model
>is good enough, you need to temper that claim by considering the
>problem size, and the scaling (and coding cost) of alternatives.  

In this case, the SMP model requires a large grid in order to scale
to 256p because it is a trivial 1D decomposition.  The good news
is that it is easy, the bad news is that it requires at least as
many planes in the domain as you have processors, and preferably
at least a few planes per processor.

These large problem sizes are relevant (albeit specialized) --
there are people who want to do high-resolution runs for the entire
continental U.S., and severe storm & tornado models will require
similar grid sizes for considerably smaller areas.

