NEW YORK (CNNfn) - Compaq Computer Corp.
                       is expected to name former TRW Inc. executive
                       Peter Hellman as its new chief executive officer
                       next week, a source close to the company told
                       CNNfn Tuesday. 
                            Hellman, who served as TRW's president and
                       chief operating officer, would fill the void left by
                       Eckhard Pfeiffer, who was ousted as Compaq's
                       CEO by the company's board in April. 
                            The world's largest personal computer maker
                       is expected to make an announcement regarding
                       Pfeiffer's replacement next week when it
                       releases its quarterly financial results. 
                            Compaq officials declined comment on
                       Hellman's appointment Tuesday. 
                            The Houston-based Compaq has struggled
                       recently amid weaker consumer demand for its
                       hallmark personal computer products. The
                       company managed to post first quarter profits
                       only half as high as analysts expected and in
                       June warned it would tally as much as a 15-cent
                       per share loss. Analysts had been expecting 20
                       cents per share profit. 
                            Compaq's stock has suffered in tandem. The
                       stock closed down 7/8 to 25-5/8 Tuesday, down
                       50 percent from its all-time high of 51-1/4
                       reached in late January. 
                            


                            Most Compaq analysts interviewed Tuesday
                       confessed to know little about Hellman, who
                       resigned his post with the Cleveland-based
                       aerospace, information-systems and automobile
                       products company in February when his position
                       was eliminated. 
                            Analysts who tracked his 10-year career at
                       TRW called Hellman, 49, a low-profile but highly
                       competent executive who consistently generated
                       a clean balance sheet and boasted a strong
                       technology background. 
                            "Their earnings have been disappointing the
                       last couple of years, but from an operating
                       perspective, clearly they were excellent," said
                       Douglas Christopher, an analyst at Crowell,
                       Weedon & Co. "TRW has always been very solid
                       in making the right strategic moves." 
                            Andrew J. Neff, an analyst at Bear Stearns in
                       New York, said Hellman's lack of computer
                       background should not be a hindrance as long
                       as he can clean up the company's balance
                       sheet, a talent he was known for at TRW. 
                            "Compaq needs a turnaround guy," Neff said.
                       "I don't know if this is the guy or not. It's too early
                       to tell. He's not a computer guy, but neither was
                       [IBM Chairman] Lou Gerstner or [new
                       Hewlett-Packard CEO] Carly Fiorina. What we're
                       looking for is a good general manager." 
                            Still, Compaq's new CEO will face other
                       challenges outside of creating efficiencies. 
                            The company is quickly losing ground to
                       fleet-footed competitors like Dell Computer Corp.
                       (DELL) and Gateway Inc. (GTW), which have
                       developed a niche in direct PC sales. Compaq
                       (CPQ), meanwhile, continues to rely on resellers
                       and retailers and has suffered from distribution
                       problems that have inflated expenses. 
                            Former CEO Pfeiffer blamed the company's
                       financial woes on an overall weakness in
                       demand for personal computers, but analysts
                       questioned his argument, noting the recent
                       success posted by Dell, Gateway and others.



