Geoff Roberts wrote:
> 
> Is there a definitive list somewhere of what (non DEC) SCSI CDROM drives
> will/will not work in a Vax/VMS system?
> I know they need to be able to cope with  512 bytes per sector, either by
> jumpering or via a SCSI mode command,
> but apart from, "this one works" "this one don't" anecdotes from users of
> same, I have yet to find a list.  I have several
> SCSI CDROMS available to me, and I'm hoping at least one can be made to talk
> to my CMD CBI-1000 VAXBI SCSI Controller.

With this particular controller, you'll find that the experiences
of folks hooking SCSI CD-ROM drives to the VMS DKDRIVER may be
nearly worthless.  Your VAX doesn't "see" a SCSI device, it sees
an emulated MSCP device, and all the SCSI details are handled by
firmware in the CMD controller, not by VMS.  You have to go to
CMD and ask them what's officially supported if you want to be sure.

In my experience, with my CMD CQD-440, every SCSI CD-ROM that I've
tried worked.  Even those that couldn't be jumpered to 512 bytes per
sector (the CMD evidently does a mode select early on.)

Hi-Tech Components (http://www.hitechcafe.com/ ) has a wide
variety of SCSI CD-ROM's available, many of them NEC's (which are
generally well regarded.)  You have to wade through their enormous
bloated web pages to see the listings of what's available,
unfortunately.

Tim. (shoppa@trailing-edge.com)



