In comp.sys.dec Geoff Roberts <geoffrob@stmarks.pp.catholic.edu.au> wrote:
> Is there a definitive list somewhere of what (non DEC) SCSI CDROM drives
> will/will not work in a Vax/VMS system?
> I know they need to be able to cope with  512 bytes per sector, either by
> jumpering or via a SCSI mode command,
> but apart from, "this one works" "this one don't" anecdotes from users of
> same, I have yet to find a list.  I have several
> SCSI CDROMS available to me, and I'm hoping at least one can be made to talk
> to my CMD CBI-1000 VAXBI SCSI Controller.
> I have looked in the VMS FAQ and found little that is useful on this
> subject.  Perhaps this could be added?

  The weird SCSI CD-ROM restrictions only apply to VMS boxes with native SCSI
interfaces, using the DKDRIVER. With a CMD controller, it will take just about
any drive you care to throw at it. I'd suggest using the fastest drive you
have laying around.

