Ohh, come on I think we both know that we are talking about
two different things. There is a  difference between
a vendor finding a specific vunerability themselves prior to any
attacks, fixing it and not reporting the vunerability as opposed to
a vendor denying or not reporting a vunerability to a known attack
which is in widespread use.

This is particulary bad in respect to denial of service attacks which
are scatter gun in nature. The attackers do not need to know the OS,
version etc of the system they are attacking simply its IP address.

You will find there are mixed views about the ethics of the
first scenario, some will want the vunerability and the fix
to be published, while others will not. This has been illustrated
fairly well by responses to the reports of OpenVMS's/UCX's
vunerability to LAND.

You will find no support for the second scenario where attacks
are allready underway and a vendor does not respond correctly.

Personnally I don't care for the security through obscurity approach
but I can see how systems managers may prefer a vunerability which
exists but which has never been exploited to be fixed but not reported.

Nor does it alter the basic point that anyone looking at the CERT
responses from Digital while the denial of service attacks were
underway would reach the conclusion that no action was required
on their part.

