I would not expect to see non UCX reports on CERT because I don't
think that any of the competing IP stack vendors are CERT
members. Obviously not all OpenVMS sites use UCX and the the
ones using other IP implementations would need to contact their
vendors directly and ask them to clarify the situtation with
respect to their stack and the various denial of service attacks.

Digital now Compaq are however CERT members and so their
responses which cover Tru64 and OpenVMS/UCX ought to
be accurate.

As to the vunerability of 3rd party IP stacks on OpenVMS,
there is a good chance that they were/are vunerable since
the denial of service attacks have had most impact on BSD
based IP stacks, which accounts for I think all 3rd party
OpenVMS stacks.

Of course the fact that we are having this discussion at
all just underlines the fallacy of OpenVMS's invunerability
to security violations. Again please note this is not a
direct attack on OpenVMS, since most UNIX OS's and
favourites like NT were also vunerable for the same
reasons, but just an illustration of the dangers of the
assumptions made by some people posting to this group
about their favourite OS.

Quite apart from the IP stack issues, there are also
generic security holes introduced by web servers
BIND etc that run on OpenVMS/UNIX/NT.



