Weeelll, OK. Not as good as the "dynamic reassignment" you get
from the scheduler inside a single SMP OS image, though.

Which logically brings me to my own private speculation about
what Galaxy is really about.

Consider the following totally mythical conversation:

Hardware guy: Hey, cool! We just figured out how to build a
128-processor shared memory system! We're going to ship on
xx/yy/zz. Got some software?

VMS kernel guy: Oh, #&(%. $@(*. %*%(. Do you know how many places
there are in the kernel where we assumed back in the early 80s,
when this thing was designed, that no SMP would *ever* get bigger
than N? [N = 8, 16, 32, some number <<128.] It's built into the
kernel's DNA, for &deity.'s sake! And the thing's NUMA, too --
more changes! Argh! What do we do?

VMSCluster guy: Ahem. Well, we've got all this nifty cluster
code. It's so good it's almost as good as a single SMP image. At
least if you squint. Hard. Anyway, how about we partition the
sucker and run VMSCluster on it? Could be pretty good if we can
carve out some memory to share for fast commo -- I've some code
right over here that does that already with Memory Channel. Can
you do that, hardware?

Hardware guy: MMmm, schedule, MMnnnn, overhead, nnMMM resources
NNNmNm schedule schedule schedule schedule...

Both software guys: No software for all those VMS customers
unless you do.

Hardware guy: No problemo.

PR Guy jumps into the room: Wow, now we can scale to infinity and
beyond! Right?! (PR guys always speak in exclamation points.)

Others: Hey, yeah, that's right. Just carve it into enough
pieces, and off you go. Great, we get rid of that problem
*forever*.

PR Guy: Great! And all these new cluster features! Dynamic
reassignment! Much faster commo than we ever had! Obviously, no
point in mentioning that we wouldn't need them if we had a single
OS image covering the whole machine!  

Others: Yup, double right.

PR Guy: Great! And VMScluster is highly available, too! First SMP
to do that!

Others: Well... Not exactly. You do get application software HA.

PR guy: Software HA! Great! Where's my
AutoMagicPowerPointExcretor?! Gotta get cracking!

All: Rationalize, rationalize, rationalize (It's what we *can*
do, so let's find all the reasons why it's what we *should* do...
Conversion experiences... Do you believe! I believe! It's RIGHT!
It's The Way! It's What We Always Should Have Done! A name, a
name, let's come up with a good name suggesting multitudes...)

ANNOUNCE GALAXY!

(In fairness, there probably is at least one person in DECompaq
who always did think that The Way Of Truth And Purity was to
support big shared-memory systems as clusters. He/She probably
also thinks big shared-memory systems are intrinsically the Work
Of The Devil, but never mind that. They exist. This is certainly
a way to support them.)

(And don't think other companies don't work exactly the same way.
It's amazing how much gets done if you can honestly decide, and
then convince others, that Virtue is exactly what's most
convenient to do.)

Greg Pfister
<clearly not an opinion attributable to my employer>



