In article <378E4EF4.8F40AC2D@us.ibm.com>, Greg Pfister <pfister@us.ibm.com> writes:
> Larry Kilgallen wrote:
>> 
>> In article <378CB06F.E2074681@us.ibm.com>, Greg Pfister <pfister@us.ibm.com> writes:
>> 
>> > Unless HA was traded off for something other than performance...
>> > In which case finding out what that "something" is would be VERY
>> > interesting.
>> 
>> Well, another Galaxy characteristic is dynamic reassignment of CPUs to
>> adjust to changing workloads, and that is not "performance" as you have
>> been using the term.
> 
> Weeelll, OK. Not as good as the "dynamic reassignment" you get
> from the scheduler inside a single SMP OS image, though.

"As good" varies according to your environment, but if you have hit
contention situations with all jobs in the same pool for critical
sections, having separate pools with separate critical sections
may ease the load.  That can be done today with two smaller SMPs
rather than one larger SMP.  Pulling the CPU boards and putting
them into the other machine is a lot of effort, even if the hardware
supports it.

So yes, everything inside the same SMP pool does make CPU reassignment
efficient (I do remember the term "time sharing"), but too many players
in the same SMP pool ("instance" in Galaxy-speak) can bring on its own
set of problems.

Larry Kilgallen



