I'm going to open mouth about matters I am not really well versed in.
My meager existence has been such that I have not seen situations where
much more than small to medium single processor VMS systems were required.

It is my understanding that the problems with scaling in a multi-CPU
system one of keeping all the processors busy, and the more processors
there are to manage, the more time the system spends on managing the
multiple processors, and more chances of the system encountering some
bottleneck.  Simplistically stated, but essentially the core of the problem.

In a VMS cluster environment there are certain synchronization objects
such as the DLM which each member has to respect, but outside of those
each processor is free to perform it's work without the overhead of a
SMP system that has been scaled beyond a point of advantage.  However,
the synchronization objects themselves could become a bottleneck that
limits the capabilities of the cluster.  The speed of inter-connects
is also another area that can constrain a cluster.

So what you speculate could be true, and I don't doubt that it occurs
in many places.  However your speculation is only one possibility, and
not necessarily a strong possibility, in a rather large pool of
possibilities.

I can speculate that the VMS people were having a real problem with
getting decent scalability with a large number of processors in a SMP
type of box, and built upon some rather obvious observations.  A SMP
system is no better, and possibly worse than a single processor system,
for a single application which cannot use multiple threads.  The SMP
system does offer advantages when running a single application where
the processing can be split, or running multiple applications.  It's
my humble opinion the VMS systems running multiple applications at the
same time vastly outnumber single process applications and thus can
benefit from more processors.

My impression of OpenVMS Galaxy is that it's a method of providing more
compute power for multiple applications in a single box, doing away with
the need for so many boxes which really contribute nothing to the computing,
and opening the way for faster communication between members of a cluster
within the same box.  It is not touted as providing better availability,
and indeed provides more single points of failure.  When the meteor comes
through the ceiling and takes out one box in a 10 box cluster, some of the
others might continue running.  When it takes out the single box with 120
CPUs and there are no other boxes...  This ignores the probability of
hitting a single box being 1/10th the probability of hitting 1 of 10 boxes,
we're saying the box WILL be hit.  But this is not bad.  If you require the
system to be disaster proof, then have the 10 or more boxes spread over the
whole planet, maybe even one on Mars in case something takes out the Earth,
but your lag times on the cluster interconnects will be interesting, and
someone's going to get real rich selling you the length of wiring you'll
need.  :-)  What OpenVMS Galaxy does is give you the capability of having
much greater capability in each box while avoiding instead of addressing the
scalability problem, and then you decide how many boxes you need and where
to locate them to address the disaster tolerant issue.  It puts you in the
situation where nothing is worse than before, and things can be a lot better
than before.

So, did they fall into this solution through luck or circumstances?  I don't
know.  But from a strategy point of view, a frontal assault is not always
the best option, and possibly the VMS people decided that avoiding the
problems of many CPUs in a SMP system would be better than trying to solve
the problem, because it is basically not solvable.  No matter how well you
solve the problem for n CPUs, there is always the case of n+1 CPUs.  Of
course, this same issue is relevant for the number of instances in a Galaxy.

I look forward to having my ignorance of very large scale computing corrected
by those who have more knowledge in this area.

Dave

