Zalman Stern wrote:
> 
> Greg Pfister <pfister@us.ibm.com> wrote:
> [Amusing possible explanation of Galaxy development.]
> 
> A sort of question about fundamentals...
> 
> Lets say you really want a large SMP because of the desireable properties
> mentioned in _In_Search_of_Clusters_ (ISoC). Namely its easy to administer
> and it handles multijob paralellism completely transparently. Also, the
> programming model is well liked. But as mentioned in ISoC, the
> synchronization issues for large SMPs are painful.  One example given is
> the hassle of maintaining exact file descriptor position semantics across a
> huge machine.

Yes. However, the desirable properties are at least as much an
issue of the way the software uses an SMP as it is a question of
the SMP hardware itself. Change the software sufficiently, and
you lose those desirable properties.

> So instead, you build software domains within the hardware and conceptually
> ship the hardware from domain to domain. Within a software domain, you get
> the exact semantics of an SMP. All that detailed synchronization happens.
> Between software domains, precise semantics are relaxed. 

Up to this point, you're describing partitioning. Including
"shipping the hardware." In addition to Galaxy, Sun does it with
dynamic domains (slowly & manually, see their docs, but they do
it); S/390 does it behind the scenes by using "logical"
partitions and a hypervisor.

> Relaxed as little
> as possible so the SMP programming model is perturbed as little as
> possible. You might even be able to do things like share memory between
> processes in different software domains.

But with this, you don't have partitions any more -- because a
key value of partitions to customers is isolation: Keeping one
from affecting the other, including crashing, in any way.

I'm not saying either partitioning or what you're saying is
wrong. They're just different paths to a similar place.

> So yeah, its a software structuring technique for making really big SMP
> hardware palatable, but it isn't just a side effect of internal politics
> and inadequate software engineering. There really is a need for another
> layer of decomposition in "the operating system" here.

Sorry if I gave the impression that it was a "squabble" by
dramatizing it. I don't consider it a mere squabble. I consider
it the emergence of a fundamental new reality of what's possible
in hardware, a reality that is in the process of blowing the legs
out from under assumptions that software has made for decades --
assumptions that lead to the "shared nothing" paradigm, among
other things.

Current system software paradigms do not fit modern huge
shared-memory machines. The fit will grow obviously, agonizingly
worse over time, as memories recede even further from processors
and, as a result, all SMPs acquire NUMA-like characteristics.

> (Of course while I think there might be a useful concept here, I'll have to
> wait until its implemented in a system I actually use before I decide
> whether there really is or not...)

Well, let's see how VMScluster performs. Hopefully they'll decide
to tell us in a way that's meaningfully comparable to other
solutions.

> Now of course the cluster answer to this is "You know, if that layer of
> decomposition was clustering done right(*), it would work over fast
> communication links between separate boxes and give you all the advantages
> of that large SMP plus high availability." Which may be true, but the the
> conceptual structure of the OS still must evolve to do that.

Yes, it must. NUMA alone tends to push the structure in that
direction, but runs the risk of making the software addicted to
direct memory access and cache coherence, which strongly limits
the availability characteristics of the system.

> -Z-
> 
> (*) Presuming there is *at least one* "right" way to do clustering :-)

Hah! Interesting assumption. I tend to think there are many right
ways, which of course infuriates people who believe there is one:
theirs.

Greg Pfister
<not my employers' opinion>



