> But if the DLM and other issues relating to multi-node
> clustering with Oracle become an inhibitor to pushing more
> transactions through the database then what do you
> do next ? partition the data and the clients to minimise
> DLM activity.
> 

Andrew, what this whole thread is quickly becoming is the age old
debate of "I don't care how you do it" and "I know exactly how I want
to do it."

Regardless of whether you are running a db on a cluster or an smp,
there is overhead associated with maintaining consistency of data.
There are multible ways to handle these issues but they still exist.  

I can construct a situation where the UE10000 is slower than an ue250
with a workload that should have infinite parallelizm.  This isn't too
hard.  It does sometimes happen in real life.  The same can be done
with a cluster.

In an SMP enviroment, a lot of unseen DLM like activity goes on behind
the scenes.  This is an issue that goes on in hardware all the time.
There are cases where an explicit coherency system would vastly out
perform the standard CC systems.  



