Jerry Leichter <leichter@smarts.com> writes:

> There's little evidence that indicates this decision was wrong.  Lock
> contention and such is a real problem when the machine gets that large. 
> One way or another, you end up having to partition your resources. 

Your limit is N=64. Your competition is building SMP machines several
times larger, and shared-less machines with simpler ideas of a single
system image are several times larger than that.

Lock contention depends on system usage, as long as you haven't
accidentally and needlessly created bottlenecks while implementing
your idea of a single system image. So no, you don't necessarily have
to partition your resource. You just have to pick the right model for
the usage your system gets, and have a system flexible enough to not
run into iself.

> (Then again, maybe a combination of
> hardware and software techniques will make full SMP's with 128 or more
> nodes practical.

You're saying this without being aware of the companies who are
shipping such products?

> It's interesting, though, that IBM, who with years of
> research on very fast interconnects is probably in a better position to
> build such a thing than almost anyone else, builds SP machines with a
> separate system image per node.)

That depends on how you define system image -- obviously you're using
"system image == system disk" at this instant. If separate system
disks gets you the functionality that you wanted, it's a good answer.

-- greg




