While literally true, this statement is so misleading it constitutes
a lie.

"more" can mean "many times as much" or it can mean "greater than by
an infinitesimal amount".  For cluster management, the latter case is
much more correct.

It probably takes a year or more to become a truly proficient VMS
system manager.  It only takes an afternoon for an experienced VMS
system manager to pick up the essentials of clustering.

Of course, a novice system manager will likely have problems and 
confusions about clustering, along with everything else.  Clusters
are just another, relatively small, subject that system managers
need to learn, along with much larger and more time-consuming topics
such as network management, backups, disk management, user account 
management, layered product installations (such as compiler upgrades), 
O/S upgrades, hardware upgrades, recognizing and dealing with hardware
faults, moving the system to New Jersey for political reasons, getting
next years budget approved, etc. etc. etc.

While your example question doesn't sound like a real question from a
real person (but maybe from a Sun-bot), it is the kind of thing a novice
manager would ask - how do I add a node to a cluster?  This is covered
in great detail in the system manager's manual, and is in most cases
quite easy.  The first response the person would receive on this news
group is "What exactly did you do?  What error message(s), exactly, did
you get?  Have you read the Fine Manual?"

Andrew, have you ever actually managed a VMS cluster, or looked over
the shoulder of someone who was carrying out typical cluster management
tasks?

</digression>
I have managed (or tried to) a number of Sun systems.  Given that they
are Unix, I don't expect to ever be able to remember the actual commands
to do anything, but I suppose native speakers of a non-western-European
language with little or no English would have the same problem with VMS.
However, I usually have to skim the entire manual set to find anything,
and the indices are almost useless.  My two most recent experiences were
a hardware upgrade for an UltraSparc 5 (adding two new disks, an external 
DLT tape drive and a bunch of memory), and setting up PPP on another
Sparc.  (Both running Solaris 2.6.)  The physical part of the hardware
install went well, though discovering the correct boot command to get
the Sun to recognize the new hardware was unaccountably obscure.  I still
haven't figured out how to tell if the Sun actually sees and is using the
new memory, but people tell me the performance is better, so I suppose it
must.  It took about 4 hours of digging through the Solaris manuals to
figure out how to do a full backup of the existing disk to the new DLT
tape drive, which I wanted to do to test the drive and to make sure we
actually had a backup of the disk.  I checked the new disk drives, and
they showed up in /dev/..., or where-ever, but I didn't do anything else
with them.  A week or so later, when some of the people at work 
(programmers) wanted to use the new disks, they couldn't find them.
Another hour tracking that down, and I'm still not sure if they managed
to deconfigure themselves or if the people were looking in the wrong
place...  The PPP setup was extremely convoluted (I've done PPP on Linux,
VMS, and PC's, so the new factor here is Solaris), but I am trying to do
something very arcane - run PPP over a telnet connection instead of over
a physical line!  The Solaris Networking manual helped a lot, but the
way Solaris does PPP is totally different from the way the Linux systems
I had previous experience with do it.  Different programs, config files,
options on ifconfig, slight variations in "route" commands, ...  I guess
VMS is VMS is VMS, but Unix isn't Unix isn't Unix.  If anyone is 
interested, I eventually got it working, but still haven't figured out
exactly what I did that was essential and what was irrelevant.  I also 
haven't figured out how to automate starting/restarting it when one of
the systems reboots.
</end digression>

My point here isn't that it is much easier to do things on VMS than on
Solaris, it is that I am an experienced VMS system manager and a novice
Sun system manager, so I find things much easier of VMS.  It is extremely
disingenuous to say that because a novice VMS manager has some (made-up)
problem with a VMS cluster, that VMS clusters are a major management
problem.

[snip]

-- 
John Santos



