In article <378670D1.FB01D40E@videotron.ca>,
JF Mezei  <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote:
>"Terry C. Shannon" wrote:
>> Note that the API 1GHz demo was done with
>> a hand-selected EV67 (0.25u) bin.
>
>Pardon my ignorance, but what exactly does this mean ?

  It came from a "hand-selected bin" .. ie, it's a specially screened-for
high-quality processor from the factory.  When processors are manufactured,
most can be run at some "expected" frequency (eg, 750MHz), the rest mostly
run at some lower (667MHz) or higher (800MHz) frequencies, and a very very
few might run stably at some other frequency (500MHz, or 900MHz, or 1GHz).
It's more or less the luck of the draw -- chance variations in the quality
of the end products.

  EV67 is a term used to refer (I think) to the fabrication technology and
the generation of the processor.  I've seen it used for years, but never 
really fully figured out the semantics of it.  My best guess is that the 
first number ("6") refers to the processor generation (21264) and the next
number ("7") refers to the fabrication technology (0.25um).  So (I think) 
EV58 would refer to a 21164PC fabricated on a 0.20um or 0.18um process.

  "0.25um" refers to the feature size the fabrication plant used to manu-
facture the processors are capable of, the most-used benchmark of how ad-
vanced the fabrication technology is.  It means (as I understand it -- 
though I'm not a hard-core EE guy) that the transistors and traces on the
processor's silicon can be "drawn" no narrower than 0.25 microns thick,
I'm not sure how it relates to the minimum distance between the traces or
transistors.  

  The smaller the transistors, the "better" the processor becomes.  It 
makes you able to put more transistors (and therefore more logic and more
cache memory) onto the chip, it makes those transistors able to switch 
more quickly (resulting in a higher clock frequency -- 750MHz, 1GHz, etc),
and it requires less power to cause them to switch, resulting in a lower
power dissipation per clock cycle (though perversely a higher operating 
temperature, since temperature increases as per the square of the clock's
frequency, and you're packing the transistors closer together, forcing 
them to dissipate that energy over a smaller area, increasing the energy
density, and thus increasing the temperature).  Halving the feature size
roughly quadruples the transistor density.  Also, making the processor 
smaller makes it less expensive, since you can put more of them on your
wafers, and your cost of running a fab plant is more or less linear with
the number of wafers you push through it.  Which is why it's been rumored
that Alpha-Processor Inc. will be selling (in addition to the big servers
and workstations) 21164PC EV58's in sub-$1000 systems, targetting the home 
user.  The 21164PC was the "small, cheap" version of the second-generation
Alpha processor, and fabbing it in 0.18um might drop the price to around 
$130 -- just about right for the home computing market.  But it's still
just a rumor.

  As I said, I'm not a hard EE guy, and a lot of this I've surmised from
reading these newsgroups for the past few years .. so take it all with an
appropriate-sized grain of salt.

  -- TTK




