> roughly quadruples the transistor density.

  Halving the feature size also means that on chip load capacitance goes
  down by nearly a factor of four (less than 4 due to fringing fields,
  increased interconnect cross section aspect ratio, and thinner gate
  oxide).  Halving the feature size also generally means reducing the
  supply voltage by two so that power consumption per MHz is reduced by
  nearly another factor of four. Compare the 0.8 um Pentium and the 0.25
  um Tillamook Pentium:

            0.8 um Pentium    0.25 um Pentium

  Die size   306 mm2             98 mm2
  Freq        66 MHz            233 MHz
  Vdd        5.0 V              1.8 V
  Power       16 W              3.9 W

  mW/MHz      242               16.7
  mW/mm2     52.3               39.8

  Please note the later Pentium is actually a more complex design
  with double the L2 caches and extra logic to implement MMX (that,
  plus bonding pad overhead, is why the 0.25 um device is not even
  smaller). The power density is less in the newer technology.

>  Also, making the processor
> smaller makes it less expensive, since you can put more of them on your
> wafers, and your cost of running a fab plant is more or less linear with
> the number of wafers you push through it.  Which is why it's been rumored
