  The hypothetical 21164PC EV58 would likely be under 80 mm2 unless
  they doubled the caches again.  Even with the more expensive 0.18 um
  wafers and the expensive alpha 413 CPGA package the part would likely
  cost well under $100 to make.  The problem here is that the 21164 core
  is bus limited for many apps even at clock rates below 500 MHz due to
  its in order execution.  A 750 MHz 0.18 um 21164PC would have little
  performance gain over a 667 MHz 0.28 um 21164PC EV57; I doubt it
  could break 20 SPECint95 with any reasonably priced external cache
  and memory system.  It might even be worthwhile scrapping the 128
  bit system bus and replacing it with separate 64 bit PC133 SDRAM bus
  with on-chip mem controller, and a PCI bus for I/O. If you figure the
  30% performance loss due to FX!32, an EV58 PC would likely run x86
code
  with lower integer performance than a 366 MHz Celeron.

  If you move away from WinNT then the hypothetical EV58 Alpha might
make
  a reasonable sub $1000 64 bit Linux box.  It might also be a decent
  processor to power the reincarnation of the Amiga (they certainly
would
  have a good story about upgrades to faster hardware ;-). By reducing
Vdd
  to 1.5 V or less and clock rate to say 500 MHz it might even meet the
  Intel 8 W design envelope for laptop computers.

