prices will go, and therefore which markets they can effectively target.

>  30% performance loss due to FX!32, an EV58 PC would likely run x86
>  code with lower integer performance than a 366 MHz Celeron.

  A better comparison would be with AMD's K6-2, which had to use off-
chip L2 clocked at the speed of the main memory bus (ie, 100MHz).  The
K6-2 was a very good performer for its time despite having only a fast 
L1.  I would guess that the hypothetical EV58 could match a (equally 
hypothetical) 600MHz K6-2's performance.

>  If you move away from WinNT then the hypothetical EV58 Alpha might
>  make a reasonable sub $1000 64 bit Linux box.  It might also be a 
>  decent processor to power the reincarnation of the Amiga (they 
>  certainly would have a good story about upgrades to faster hardware 
>  ;-). By reducing Vdd to 1.5 V or less and clock rate to say 500 MHz 
>  it might even meet the Intel 8 W design envelope for laptop computers.

  :-)  It would be nice.  But it remains to be seen if Linux will become
a significant force in the home computer market, and business users are 
not a significant player in the sub-$1k market.  Not that I wouldn't mind
having one (or a few) myself, but if the money isn't there the product
will never see production.  I also don't think Amiga is going with Alpha,
since they were previously planning on using QNX for their underlying OS
technology, and I do not think QNX was ever ported to Alpha.

  OTOH, I think the EV58 *would* make a decent Windows platform.  As I
said (unabashedly speculated), performance would be very inconsistent
between applications, but the same is true of the cache-anemic Celerons.
That has been no roadblock to its success in the home market.

>>   As I said, I'm not a hard EE guy, and a lot of this I've surmised from
>> reading these newsgroups for the past few years .. so take it all with an
>> appropriate-sized grain of salt.

