[snip]
> 
> >  It might even be worthwhile scrapping the 128
> >  bit system bus and replacing it with separate 64 bit PC133 SDRAM bus
> >  with on-chip mem controller, and a PCI bus for I/O. If you figure the
> 
>   I would be interested in seeing what a 512KB L3 cache running at the
> bus' full 200MHz would do for the hypothetical EV58's performance.  The
> difference between a 1200MHz processor and 200MHz L3 would only be six
> wait-states.  (And I do think 1200MHz is a more realistic figure than
> 750MHz for 0.18um -- the more complex (and harder to clock) 0.25um EV67
> is already clocking at 750MHz.)

   I would be careful about using complexity as a guide to clock rate
   between two CPU implementations as different as the 21164 and 21264.
   The 21264 is the most complex out of order machine yet disclosed yet
   the microarchitecture was thoughtfully and carefully designed to
allow
   high clock rates.  In contrast the 21164 CPU core was designed with
   the goal of reducing many of the latencies of the 21064 and has two
   major critical speed paths related to D-cache accesses.

   I agree that a 21164PC in 0.18 um would likely be able to go faster
   than 750 MHz but 1 GHz isn't a gimme due to its relatively short
pipes.
   Also the purpose of a 21164PC in 0.18 um would be to achieve low cost
   and not necessarily cosmic clock rates.  The clock rate might also be
   capped to reduce Vdd and power consumption to permit less expensive
   packaging (the 21164PC CPGA package would likely cost more than an
   0.18 um 21164PC known good die)


[snip]
