   OTOH, it was said that Amiga wouldn't be x86 based.  A likely
   guess would be PowerPC since a QNX port to it was recently
   disclosed.  OTOOH, maybe the original CPU was ditched along
   with QNX when the decision was made to switch to Linux.

> 
>   OTOH, I think the EV58 *would* make a decent Windows platform.  As I
> said (unabashedly speculated), performance would be very inconsistent
> between applications, but the same is true of the cache-anemic Celerons.
> That has been no roadblock to its success in the home market.

   I think the idea that second tier (i.e. low cost) Alphas can succeed
   in the WinNT market is very suspect.  Given Moore's law, a second
   tier Alpha + FX!32 will almost always be slower than the latest x86
   in running x86 code.  Given the economic realities of the low channel
   overhead, high volume production, and fierce competition in the x86
   world, a second tier Alpha box will likely always be noticably more
   expensive.  Personnaly I think 32 bit Win NT is so biased towards x86
   that it is a swamp even for high end Alphas.

   Also IMHO any devlopment efforts towards 21164PC and other deliber-
   ately second tier Alphas that takes resources away from the effort
   of keeping flagship Alpha parts as far in the performance lead as
   possible is ill spent.  Shrink and cost reduce the 21264 as much
   as possible sure but don't burn up man years of scarce engineering
   resources making a crippled version of the design.


All opinions strictly my own.
