> operating systems. The way I see it, the Galaxy engineers seem quite proud of
> the fact that they have managed to partition a system into multiple instances
> without having code that runs below each instance and instead relying on each
> instance to be a good citizen.

Well, I was thinking something a lot lower-level than a monitor - say,
a bit that tells the memory crossbar switch not to route any writes from 
bus "A" to bus "B". I'd guess something like this exists in today's
partitionable systems, and there's no reason for a Galaxy not to take 
advantage of similar features on future AlphaServers.

Resources may be blocked by the switch, but there's no virtualization, 
and no extra level of indirection. I'd still claim that there's no 
"code running below each instance" in this case. I guess you need to 
define "code"...

