In article <37863386.41300FC2@tsoft-inc.com>,
David A Froble  <davef@tsoft-inc.com> wrote:

>For that matter, why isn't Intel looking at Alpha.  With the capability of
>loading PAL code to alter the 'appearance' of the CPU, it might be possible
>to have an Alpha chip run software written for the Intel 32-bit chips.  I'm
>a bit fuzzy about this, but I seem to remember some discussion of it when the
>Alpha was first introduced.

The reason HP and and Intel are committed to IA-64 is very simple:
because it provides hardware support for PA-RISC and IA-32, at high
performance levels. You may not support backwards compatibility from a
technical point of view, but from a marketing point of view, companies
who are good at mantaining backwards compatibility (IBM, especially,
and also Intel and Microsoft) have been dominant in their respective
fields, where those that don't (Apple and DEC, for example) have been
less successful, since the architecture is always changing.

I don't know if Alpha can fill the backwards compatibility with PA-RISC
and IA-32. I don't know much about PAL code, but I understand that
Merced is going to boot up in IA-32 real mode. Would PAL code be
available that early in the boot process to e.g. boot MS-DOS, and run
at performance levels equivalent to the newest IA-32 processor? Would
it provide high enough performance and be able to run both IA-32 and
PA-RISC instruction sets AND switch between them seamlessly?

Sure, Alpha is fast, but it runs an ISA that 99% of the world doesn't
care about. If Alpha could run IA-32 that fast, it would sell, and would
be a serious option for Intel, but not before that.

-- Terry



