I'd have to say then that your point completely escapes me:  the times of
DEC's greatest success were during and shortly after this proliferation of
architectures and OSs, which while very successful in their own right could
be considered each to have contributed their best elements to VAX and VMS,
which DEC then rode to being the only company considered to have a chance of
overtaking IBM (given their comparative growth rates through the mid-80s).

DEC became less successful only after standardizing its architecture around
VMS (plus, a bit later, Unix as a second market), and I don't believe that's
entirely coincidence:  without all the intra-company friendly (mostly)
rivalry, and for at least quite a while lacking serious external
competition, development of the core system slackened, at least somewhat,
while the company expended huge amounts of resources developing vertical
products of widely-varying competence.

In other words, lacking the impetus to continue evolving its core
competency, it largely lost its sense of direction.  I fail to see where
compatibility had anything to do with it.





