> 
> >For that matter, why isn't Intel looking at Alpha.  With the capability of
> >loading PAL code to alter the 'appearance' of the CPU, it might be possible
> >to have an Alpha chip run software written for the Intel 32-bit chips.  I'm
> >a bit fuzzy about this, but I seem to remember some discussion of it when the
> >Alpha was first introduced.
> 
> The reason HP and and Intel are committed to IA-64 is very simple:
> because it provides hardware support for PA-RISC and IA-32, at high
> performance levels.

   To the extent that Merced reaches "high performance levels" it won't
   be with PA-RISC code and it certainly won't be with IA-32 code.
   The PA-RISC support will involve binary recompilation in a similar
   way as DEC supported porting MIPS code to Alpha.  The IA-32 support
   is in hardware but it's nothing to get excited about.  Last October
   Intel was saying that Merced x86 performance would be equivalent to
   a "mainstream" x86 processor shipping when Merced does.

   Since that pronouncement three things happened 1) x86 performance has
   accelerated faster than year old roadmaps due to good old fashioned
   competition, 2) the Merced has slipped schedule even further (As Mr
   Gwennap of MDR so nicely puts it, Merced performance has to increase
   4% each month it slips just to maintain its competitive position),
   and 3) there are many indications that Intel will have difficulty
   reaching their target frequency (often given as 800 MHz).  A year
   ago what would Intel have assumed a "mainstream" x86 processor in
   1H00 be? Maybe something around 20 SPECint95 and 15 SPECfp95?

> You may not support backwards compatibility from a
> technical point of view, but from a marketing point of view, companies
> who are good at mantaining backwards compatibility (IBM, especially,
> and also Intel and Microsoft) have been dominant in their respective
> fields, where those that don't (Apple and DEC, for example) have been
> less successful, since the architecture is always changing.
> 
> I don't know if Alpha can fill the backwards compatibility with PA-RISC
> and IA-32. I don't know much about PAL code, but I understand that
> Merced is going to boot up in IA-32 real mode. Would PAL code be
> available that early in the boot process to e.g. boot MS-DOS, and run
> at performance levels equivalent to the newest IA-32 processor? Would
> it provide high enough performance and be able to run both IA-32 and
> PA-RISC instruction sets AND switch between them seamlessly?
> 
> Sure, Alpha is fast, but it runs an ISA that 99% of the world doesn't
> care about. If Alpha could run IA-32 that fast, it would sell, and would
> be a serious option for Intel, but not before that.

   This fall the EV67 at 750 MHz will be yielding native performance
   around 43 SPECint95 and 78 SPECfp95.  The FX!32 recompiler is
   generally acknowledged to run translated IA-32 binaries at around
   70% of native Alpha integer performance and about 50% of native
   Alpha floating point performance.  That means the 750 MHz Alpha
   EV67 could in theory execute x86 binaries at equivalent performance
   of around 30 SPECint95 and 40 SPECfp95.  This is more like a 1 GHz
   K7 than the estimated 20 SPECint95 and 15 SPECfp95 that a Merced
   might get running x86 code in compatibility mode *if* it reaches
   its target clock rate.  How a 1+ GHz EV68 (which Merced will be
   facing) performs running x86 code with FX!32 I leave as an exercise
   for the reader  ;-)

   My 20/15 SPEC95 estimate for Merced x86 performance is arguable
   (especially the 15 SPECfp95) but it is likely that Alpha + FX!32
   will easily outpace Merced x86 performance and even stack up
   well with high x86 CPUs.

> 
> -- Terry

All opinions strictly my own.
