The AMD deal **did** fall through, but I doubt it fell through because
DEC didn't want to manufacture parts... recall that AMD was going
through a rough patch at the time they backed out of the deal.

What burns me about DEC Semi were the blatant lies they told about fab
utilization. Based on what DEC Semi marketing and management flacks
said, utilization was greater than 60 percent and break-even was "jsut
around the corner."

Well, the light at the end of the DEC Semi tunnel proved to be the
headlamp of an oncoming locomotive... after the fab was sold, DEC
executives (including Bruce Claflin) said utilization never even reached
30 percent.

IMHO the fab never should have been built in the first place. The
decision to build the fab (which, I believe, predates Palmer's
ascendency to the throne) was predicated on incredible volumes of Alpha
chips. These volumes were based on early (1990-1991) plans to build tons
of low-cost Alpha chips for PCs and X-terminals and heaven knows what
else. These plans and the related numbers were bogus.

