JF Mezei wrote:
> 
> "Terry C. Shannon" wrote:
> > Note that the API 1GHz demo was done with
> > a hand-selected EV67 (0.25u) bin.
> 
> Pardon my ignorance, but what exactly does this mean ?

   If you fabricate a large number of chips and plotted the
   distribution of maximum clock frequency for each part
   you will get a pretty good bell curve.  This yield curve
   results from normal manufacturing variations primarily in
   transistor gate length, gate oxide thickness, and transistor
   threshold voltage (the curve itself isn't fixed but will
   change over time - it will get narrower as the process is
   better understood and SPC tightened up.  And as the curve
   narrows it can be shifted to higher frequencies without
   the risk of creating parts with long term reliability
   problems)  Since fast chips can be sold for more money,
   during final test it is typical for parts to be divided
   up into two or three frequency ranges - figuratively
   placed into separate "bins"

   In the case of the 0.25 um EV67, a part that operates at
   1 GHz would be pretty rare - an outlyer on the fast edge
   of the yield bell curve, certainly rare enough that it
   wouldn't be worth while setting up a bin for that speed.
   To find the magic 1 GHz EV67 would have required a special
   screening exercise and hence the expression hand selected.
   For all I know Samsung could have even run a special wafer
   batch with all the process parameters skewed to generate
   a handful of very fast parts.

   The significance of the demo is 1) the EV67 probably has
   tons of headroom for the announced 667 and 750 MHz speed
   grades and will have high AC yields, and 2) the 0.18 um
   EV68 probably either hasn't seen first silicon or been
   evaluated closely enough yet to risk a public demo (or if
   you have Grovian levels of paranoia you might infer that
   Samsung wanted to demo a 1GHz part while concealing the
   status of the EV68  ;-)


All opinions strictly my own.
