> Burns Fisher wrote:
> [big snip]
>> It all comes down to this:  We have NEVER claimed Galaxy to increase
>> availability.  This is not just for mixed operating systems (which, you
>> realize,
>> we don't even officially support) but for pure VMS Galaxy systems.
> 
> So you get all the disadvantages of clusters - partitioning data,
> system management headaches, much less third-party software
> support, etc. - and throw away one of the key advantages.
> 

	Greg, I think you mean: "all the disadvantages of Unix clusters".
	I believe you left out a word there.  Not to drag things down
	too much but here is an exchange from 4/23/98 that Andrew Harrison was
	involved in and quickly sidestepped (trimming where appropriate,
	do a Deja search and read it all).  Note that Andrew has 
	tried to pull the "partitioning" and "complexity" cards before.  

	You're in select company there.   :-O 

---- 

Andrew writes:

Providing you persuade customers to swallow the
costs of the additional complexity of managing
a cluster.

Providing you persuade customers to swallow the
costs of re-architecting their database to make
best use of the cluster (partitioning databases)
etc.

To which D. Webb responds:

 1) A cluster ( VMS style ) is usually configured as a single management
                    domain.
    It is practically as simple to manage as a single system.
	One system disk.
    	One User authorization file.
    	Clusterwide installation of most software ( some thirdparties who 
	don't know enougth about clustering may require you too install
	on more than one node but 99% install clusterwide).
    	Clusterwide print and batch queues.
    	Clusterwide system startup file.
    	Clusterwide safe access to all files and disks via
	distributed lock manager.
    	Clusterwide mail access and mail notification.

                     
 2) Database vendors such as Oracle have already done all the work you
    need to support clustering and this does not involve 'partitioning databases'.
    The database is just as you would set it up for a
    single node.
    The only difference is that each system has to startup
    its own instance to access the database - this has it's own rollback
    segments and redo logs.
    However the instances on the two or more clustered systems access
    exactly the same database files and use the distributed lock
    manager to co-ordinate this access.

----

	So while many large Unix shops employ DBAs to move tables
	around so as to not saturate a node or interconnect, a true
	shared filesystem doesn't have that disadvantage.  Yes, the
	IO scaling is much harder and costlier (and maybe not as attainatble
	yet as Andrew would be quick to point out a UE10000 has 1 Gig/sec
	IO bandwidth) but larger FC switching is coming for large clusters.

	Let's put to rest cluster disadvantages as far as VMS are concerned
	and might I point out that Tru64 has/is gaining many of the 
	VMS cluster features (shared filesystem, single boot device).

>
> Galaxy must really provide some amazing performance numbers for
> this to be the right tradeoff.  When are we going to start seeing
> some benchmarks? I couldn't find any on the TPC web site when I
> searched for "VMS."
> 
> Greg Pfister


	Nice cheap dig.  Just wait... you know you are going to see
	scaling you haven't seen before don't you?  After all, you
	know where the bottlenecks lie and what you would like to do
	to get around them.

	By the way, how many boot devices does a 512 node SP require?

				Rob




