Sorry, I couldn't let this stand, 
Rob, I think you mean "all the disadvantages of *traditional* Unix clusters".
Perhaps you should take a look at UnixWare NonStop Clusters (derived via
a long chain of inheritance form the original UCLA Locus project), which 
isn't just (as D. Webb said) "practically as simple to manage as a 
single system" it is as simple to manage as a single system, since a 
single system image is what is presented to the user/sysadmin/programmer.
Highlights include:
	- clusterwide root filesystem image (one /etc/passwd etc.)
	- clusterwide file and filesystems namespace and access
	- clusterwide device namespace and access
	- clusterwide process namespace and /proc, with processes migration
	  and loadlevelling
	- clusterwide IPC (including: semaphores, pipes, shared memory)
	- clusterwide networking and port space (any node in the cluster
	  can use any external network interface on any other node, and
	  can bind/listen on any port - there are extensions to allow
	  multiple nodes to bind to the same port (e.g. port 80) with 
	  loadbalancing between nodes)
	- single system semantics for users/sysadmin/programmers
	- a few simple extensions for "cluster-aware" applications

This is all done whilst maintaining the *traditional* Unix clusters'
high availability characteristics (no single point of failure, any node can 
fail and the cluster keeps on running - you will loose resources that were
only available on that node - e.g. devices attached only to that node)

See the paper "Implementing a Full Single System Image UnixWare Cluster: 
Middleware vs Underware" by Bruce Walker and Douglas Steel, PDPTA'99 
http://www.dgs.monash.edu.au/~rajkumar/pdpta99/douglass.ps.gz

or the marketing blurb at http://www.sco.com/products/clustering

Douglas Steel - I speak only for me, not for SCO



