If we are going to be pedantic.

Sun renamed SunOS 4.x.x  Solaris 1.x when Solaris 2.0 was released but this
does not refer to the kernel just the complete package including X, Openwindows
etc. I have in my cupboard a CD set labeled SunOS 4.1.3 because it pre-dates
the release of Solaris 2.x.

The kernel is still called SunOS and uname -a on a Solaris 2.x or 7 package will
report SunOS "machine name" "SunOS version".

When someone says that a system is BSD of System V based they are normally
refering to the kernel since it has been common practice for most vendors to ship

BSD utilities for their System V based system or System V utilities for their BSD

based system.

However most users and administrators refer to all Sun operating systems
releases before Solaris 2.0 as SunOS and all releases after 2.x as Solaris
and that was what I was refering to.

All the releases prior to 2.x were BSD based, all the releases after were
mainly System V based.

Regards
