In article <378AD403.9A790D15@sodapop.org>, John Gray <johng@sodapop.org> writes:
> Jeff Sullivan wrote:
> 
>> I think you need to install the Kernel Header and Common Files subset.
>> That should be OSFBINCOMnnn, where nnn relates to the version of the
>> OS that you are running (nnn=425 for V4.0D, for example).

>   Thanks for the info but I don't have a DEC work station.  Just the
> driver source
>    and driver specific header files.

Tru64 Unix (nee Digital Unix (nee DEC OSF/1)) is different from Linux
and OpenBSD in one irrefutable regard -- it is not free.  It is licensed
software that may only be used on a machine licensed to run it.  Since
your machine is not an Alpha, I believe it is impossible for you to even
buy a license for Tru64 on your machine (where it would not work) just
to use the header files under discussion.

This does not seem incompatible with DEQ's business goals, which include
selling Alpha machines.  It may seem more incompatible with their goals
if Tru64 is released for Intel IA64 machines, which if and when they come
will be available from multiple manufacturers.  At that time, presumably
DEQ will be willing to license Tru64 for various manufacturer's IA64
machines, but still not for your incompatible machine.

Clearly it is technically possible to do something which is not legally
permitted, but those of us who make a living in the building and selling
of software are not much interested in helping cheat some other vendor
out of their revenue.

Larry Kilgallen



