Rob, in all the discussions we have had you have never
sucessfully shown how an OpenVMS based Parallel
DBMS cluster with say 2 nodes and one copy of the
database differs from a UNIX cluster in terms of
scalability. In both cases one would use Oracle, in
both cases Oracle would use a DLM optimised by the
platform vendor to minimise its latency and system
overhead.

But in both cases on high volume lock intensive
transactions, where the nodes are accessing the
same blocks the DLM becomes an inhibitor to the
system scaling as you add more nodes.

I know lots of people buy clusters for reliability, but
many people also use clusters because the largest
node from their chosen vendor isn't capable of delivering
enough throughput into a single database. This
is of course where we get back to the DLM.

I would be interested in your examples of how OpenVMS
will automagically ensures that the node/data access is
partitioned form the other nodes and how client queries
a directed to the correct node supporting the subset of
the data.

OpenVMS has better clustering solutions than most
but anyone who tries to suggest that a clustered
system is the same complexity to own and manage
as a single node is destined for a great future in WolfPack
marketing at MS. You only have to read comp.os.vms
and see the threads like "I cannot register my 4100
with the cluster" to understand this.

If my memory is correct at about this point you vanished
into a discussion about how Spiralog would help this. I
hope I don't need to remind you that Spiralog delivered
all the benefits you mentioned but only in the origional
Digital white papers, real experience with it was rather
different.

We can all find examples of database applications that have
minimal lock activity and which scale beautifully on a cluster
but this is because of the design and access patern into the
DBMS. This is not because it is clustered on OpenVMS
or UNIX.

I would be very interested in real examples of OpenVMS
clusters with real OLTP applications based on say Oracle
that use more than one node in the cluster to run the
DBMS processes with a single instance of the DBMS
data shared by those nodes. I would also be interested
in your reasons for CPQ not having published any TPC-C
numbers using a clustered OpenVMS system given
that it's apparently so easy to do on OpenVMS.

Either CPQ is interested in providing customers
with some evidence if only with TPC-C that OpenVMS
clusters scale, or they arn't. If they arn't why not
do we just have to take their word for it or should they
just go out an buy a Sun, IBM(Sequent) or HP box.

<snip>
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect






