In article <3783B10F.38D22B7C@world.std.com>, "Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:

Any company stupid enough to cozy up to Microsoft always gets it in the
aff.  DEC got it in the aff from Microsoft so often that it seemed like an
infinite number of times.  aff + infinity = affinity.  Simple, really. 

>Compaq has learned the hard way that NT-on-Alpha did not fly, and that
>NT-VMS "Affinity" was not well received by the VMS base.

Probably because there was little or nothing there to receive.  The only
Affinity that would have mattered for most of us would have been for
the following to have been added to base OpenVMS systems (ie, not an extra 
cost add on.)

1.  SMB server capability (At least for a small number of connections.) 

2.  SMB client capability (For printer access, remote mount and copying
    applications.) 

3.  Wind/U type of windows->OpenVMS software conversion.

Problem was, Affinity didn't add any of this in a real way.  1 and 2 are
available standard on Windows systems, yet 1 on OpenVMS requires Pathworks
/Advanced Server (or Samba).  The first option is very expensive compared to
Windows solutions, and the second is not supported.  (2) is only marginally
available now via smbclient from Samba (does Advanced Server have this
capability???).  (3) was always a joke, and is now more so.  Sure, we got
Bristol's run time libraries, but the development tools were outrageously
expensive, and there were run time license costs to actually use the
libraries.  In light of Bristol's current difficulties with Microsoft, it
seems that this "option" is about to vaporize in any case. 

