Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:

[a lot of stuff that will be interpreted by some as somewhat
offensive advocacy, most of which I've tried to delete from here,
with some significant questions interleaved that I've tried to
retain.]

> 
> Rob Young wrote:
[stuff snipped]

what about performance scaling where

> ... the DLM becomes an inhibitor to the
> system scaling as you add more nodes.

Not just the DLM (Distributed Lock Manager). The data transfers
required for coherence between the separate DB caches can be an
inhibitor, too, potentially mitigated by the "Cache Fusion"
facilities in Oracle 8i.

[snip]

> We can all find examples of database applications that have
> minimal lock activity and which scale beautifully on a cluster
> but this is because of the design and access patern into the
> DBMS. This is not because it is clustered on OpenVMS
> or UNIX.

Absolutely. Business intelligence is the prime example of this.

> ... I would also be interested
> in your reasons for CPQ not having published any TPC-C
> numbers using a clustered OpenVMS system given
> that it's apparently so easy to do on OpenVMS.

Careful here, and not just about contentious advocacy.

The way the TPC-C benchmark is defined, the cluster version *IS*
a case of minimal cross-system lock activity. It's really two
different benchmarks, one for non-clusters and one for clusters,
which happen to share a name.

With the advocacy removed, the above is exactly the information
I've been fishing for. Is there data showing that Galaxy works
better than, say, systems connected by ServerNet or a VIA
implementation or an IBM high-speed switch or MyriaNet or ...?

It doesn't have to be TPC-C benchmarks. There are certainly
precedents for vendors not liking that benchmark because it
doesn't demonstrate their strengths. (S/390 shuns them, too, for
that reason.)

But there ought to be *some* data. Using the shared memory
appears to have traded off high availability for something; the
interconnects mentioned above maintain HA. The most obvious
"something" to have traded HA off for is performance. Data
demonstrating those performance benefits would be of significant
interest to many.

Unless HA was traded off for something other than performance...
In which case finding out what that "something" is would be VERY
interesting.

Greg Pfister
<not my employers' opinion>



