Andy Glew wrote:
> 
> Could one OS instance misprogram a DMA I/O, so that the I/O device
> would corrupt another instance's memory?

Yep, DMA is a possible point of failure on current hardware. A wild DMA 
can write to other instances' private memory, since all instances share 
the same memory bus. I can imagine some sort of hardware protection 
against this on a future platform with a different memory architecture, 
but for now DMA is a weak point.

> (a) an extra level of mapping - virtual memory, mapped to "processor physical
>     memory", mapped to "Galaxy physical memory" addresses.

This is not possible with Galaxy, since by design, nothing is virtualized.
"Galaxy physical memory" IS physical memory. Any protection against
wild DMA writes would have to be hardware-assisted in some way.

