In article <3784E619.4B902F4E@us.ibm.com>, Greg Pfister <pfister@us.ibm.com> writes:
> Burns Fisher wrote:
> [big snip]
>> It all comes down to this:  We have NEVER claimed Galaxy to increase
>> availability.  This is not just for mixed operating systems (which, you
>> realize,
>> we don't even officially support) but for pure VMS Galaxy systems.
> 
> So you get all the disadvantages of clusters - partitioning data,
> system management headaches, much less third-party software
> support, etc. - and throw away one of the key advantages.

What do you mean by "partitioning data" ?  Since you wrote a book on
the subject, I presume you don't mean disk data.  If you are talking
about memory data, that is one regard in which Galaxy is a significant
change from ordinary clusters.

Third party support ?  What "support" do you want ?  Anything that runs
on VMS normally will run on a Galaxy with the only difference being the
ability to bring more CPUs to bear.  That, of course, is not useful for
single process nonthreaded applications, but not every application has
that characteristic.

I view clustering on my tiny cluster as a benefit, not a liability.
All files are accessible from all machines, Alpha and VAX.  There are
no particular system management headaches.

Someone who has uptime as a prime goal certainly wants three boxes,
but a site that has 53 boxes might view 3 as an improvement, with no
decrease in availability.

Larry Kilgallen



