:Burns Fisher wrote:
:[big snip]
:> It all comes down to this:  We have NEVER claimed Galaxy to increase
:> availability.  This is not just for mixed operating systems (which, you
:> realize,
:> we don't even officially support) but for pure VMS Galaxy systems.
:
:So you get all the disadvantages of clusters - partitioning data,
:system management headaches, much less third-party software
:support, etc. - and throw away one of the key advantages.

  Instances of an OpenVMS Galaxy can be configured as members of an 
  OpenVMS Cluster, with all manner of mixtures among OpenVMS Galaxy
  instances and external OpenVMS systems possible.

:Galaxy must really provide some amazing performance numbers for
:this to be the right tradeoff.  When are we going to start seeing
:some benchmarks? I couldn't find any on the TPC web site when I
:searched for "VMS."

  I would not expect to see much in the way of benchmarks as yet, as there 
  are no platforms designed for OpenVMS Galaxy available yet -- there are 
  certainly existing platforms where it can be useful and can operate on
  and can be useful on, but the design center for the work is for (far) 
  larger numbers of processors than are currently available.  The OpenVMS 
  Galaxy work targets limitations that can and do result from SMP scaling,
  something that doesn't become a performance factor until the contention 
  that arises from reaching some application-specific number of processors.
  (Visit one of the newsgroup search engines and look for the discussion -- 
  involving Professor Minsky and a conjecture of his -- that transpired a 
  while back over in comp.os.vms...)

  OpenVMS Galaxy can also tend to be useful in "political environments", 
