By 'partitioning data', I assume you mean in memory - compared with the
ability to operate on shared data in traditional ways (i.e., ways that don't
have to worry about what might happen if the data is left corrupted by one
processor's failure but remains accessible to other processors) in SMPs.  If
you write-share memory between processors in Galaxy, you pretty much need a
new way of handling it.

It would certainly be nice if Galaxy did provide real memory separation when
desired, and in fact it could be programmed into the 'big' switch or fabric
that connects the QBBs (if that's the right TLA), just like SAN fabrics can
determine who gets to talk to whom.  Then a Galaxy *would* provide fault
isolation equivalent to a cluster (well, if the fabric itself was redundant,
anyway), and thus have all the advantages of a cluster with much faster
communication than is currently available - plus its ability to shuffle
processors and other resources around on demand.

Lacking such isolation, Galaxy is a bit betwixt and between:  a bit
intrinsically slower than an SMP given the special mechanisms necessary to
protect shared memory from partial failures plus the normal added overheads
associated with cluster cooperation, but with the increased potential
scalability of a cluster and really fast communication between cluster nodes
in the same box.  And there's *some* increased availability over an SMP,
since only a small percentage of possible faults can affect shared memory in
ways that the mechanisms used to coordinate it won't handle.

As long as one can cluster multiple Galaxy boxes together to achieve really
high availability without having the existence of the inter-box connection
compromise in-box communication performance (either directly, or indirectly
simply due to the existence of external cluster members - though for things
like lock management as long as all concurrent accessors can be localized
within a single box existing algorithms should prevent this from being a
problem), more limited availability in the single-box configuration, while
less than ideal, doesn't seem a crippling problem.






