We had a similar problem recently with our Alpha/NT servers and
StorageTek 9490 tape drives with autoloaders.  We kept getting data
errors reported from our application when reading from the tape
drives.  This happened even though the operators were cleaning the
drives when prompted by the cleaning light.  Our initial solution was
to shorten the time for the cleaning light to come on.  This helped
some, but still didn't solve the problem. 

At some point of high frustration, the hardware guys hooked up a SCSI
analyzer to the bus to see if we could determine what was happening.
It turned out that the tape drive was notifying the OS that the
cleaning light was on in a non-typical way which still met the SCSI
standard.  If this happened at just the right time, such as when the
autoloader was empty and a tape load was requested, NT fails to
identify the extended SCSI error code and just tells the application
that a data error has occurred.  

The easy solution was to make sure the autoloader is never empty.  Our
solution was to write a driver for the 9490 tape drive which checked
the extended error code for the cleaning light error, and correctly
returned it as "drive not ready" instead of data error.

Not to say that dirt isn't your problem, but just wanted to let you
know there are some interesting features in NT that manifest
themselves in  very strange ways.

Bob



