You are right HP currently has PA and Intel ranges with MPE/HP-UX
NT/SCO and Linux but they have a plan to change this. Alpha fans
may think its a bad plan because it means eventually dropping HP-PA
in favour of IA-64 but that is what HP is working towards. HP have
taken the first steps towards this with the N4000 which currently uses
HP-PA but which can be upgraded to IA-64.

They do currently sell SCO but with the advent of IA-64 and the HP-UX
port to IA-64 they will be focusing on HP-UX and not SCO and my guess
is that customers will have to load SCO/Monterey themselves if they
want it.

HP can be accused of glossing over the issues of their IA-64 transition
so as not to scare customers but once it has happened they will have
two common architectures x86 and IA-64 with some upward compatibility
from x86 to IA-64.

How much HP has to invest in supporting Oracle RAW depends on if
Oracle meet their design goals for the product. If they do then HP won't
have to invest very much, if they don't then this could increase.

>
> Fujitsu/SNI have a dizzying array of Mainframes, Unix RISC workstations
> and servers, PCs running MS, Unix and I would guess Linux.
>

Actually Fujitsu/SNI have one range of mainframes. SNI/ICL and Amdahl all
OEM their mainframes from Fujitsu. ICL and SNI provide their own OS's
while Amdahl uses MVS. This was one of the selling points behind the deal.

For the mid range SNI and Fujitsu are both Solaris licencees and produce
currently x86 and SPARC based machines moving to IA-64 and SPARC
based machines.

I am not really sure how this is relevant to CPQ, the bald facts are that
CPQ at a time of squeezed margins and increasing competition is trying
to keep more OS/Platform balls in the air than any other vendor with the
possible exception of IBM.

The Fujitsu/SNI merge is a plan to reduce the number of balls they are
juggling, the IBM Montery/AS400 RS/6000 merge + the move to cross-platform
Java development is part of IBM's plan and HP migrating to IA-64 is HP's.

Don't you begin to find a common thread in all this, isn't it strange that
only CPQ of all the major platform vendors thinks that it is viable to
be increasing the number of plaforms they support.

> It's not what Compaq is supporting, but so what?  Sun stands out among
