> In comp.arch Burns Fisher <fisher@nospam.skylab.enet.dec.com> wrote:
> : 3) If an OS has shared memory, it can then have code to implement the
whole
> : Galaxy Software Archecture stuff which includes stuff like membership
> : services, locking services, shared memory management, etc.  All this
stuff
> : (mostly) does not have any console intervention.
>
> I'm guessing that by "shared memory" you mean shared between different
OSes
> that are members of the Galaxy. (As opposed to say shared by processes
that
> are running under a single OS.)

Yes, that is correct.  Shared memory, in the Galaxy context, is a technical
term.
When you set up a soft-partitioned system, you get to define how much
memory goes to each instance for private memory, and how much goes to
memory shared among all the instances.


>I would guess that there is some support in
> the console for managing allocation of such shared memory.

Yes, with current systems at least, you (the system mgr) specify
how much memory for each instance and how much for shared.  The
console actually allocates the physical addresses among these
different categories.

> Is there support
> in the console for managing allocation within the shared memory itself and
> finding certain key data structure or is this linked into each OS
instance?

No, this is not the console's job.  This is the job of the software
that I talked about in the previous post; the software that implements
the Galaxy software architecture.  This software knows how to keep
data structures relating to the shared memory in the shared memory
itself.  It also knows how to do locking between the instances, and how
to combine these things to allocate and access shared memory.

Burns Fisher
VMS Engineering






