JF Mezei <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote in message
news:377BECD4.8E3A15DD@videotron.ca...

> If you tell everyone what they should and should NOT have at boot time and
> then leave everyone to do their own business (potentially exchanging some
bits
> now and then), what is there to prevent one OS instance from negatively
> affecting another OS instance ?

Yes, you are right.  There is nothing to prevent one OS from interfering
with the
other except having the OSes written correctly.  The same is true of
processes
within VMS.  There is nothing to keep them from interfering with each other
except that the OS is written correctly :-)

> Is it correct to state that a CPU can be onwed by a single instance ? (and
> thus, no other instance can crash it ?).

You mean that another instance can't "crash" a CPU.  I believe that this is
true.  One instance can't "get at" a CPU until the console give the CPU to
it.
There may be some trickery deep in the bowels of the console that, if an OS
duplicated, might allow it to shanghai a processor, but I don't know that.

> But is there anything which prevents one instance from screwing with
another
> instance's physical memory ?

It comes down to only one thing:  Correct programming.  However, with purely
VMS instances, there are two different mistakes that would have to happen
before
one could write into the other:  First, the rogue would have to map some
virtual
address to the physical address owned by the victim.  Secondly, it would
have to
write to that virtual address.  Not too likely for both to happen by
accident.

I guess one could also do a store physical, but that is unlikely to happen
by
accident either.  Store physical is not used that often in VMS.

> Suppose you have an NT instance and a VMS instance, what is there to
prevent
> the NT instance from writing its own garbage into the VMS memory space
(thus
> causing VMS to have a severe indigestion ?)
> Is this a question of trust ?

Sure, in the end that is what it comes down to.  I know little about the
internals of
NT.  However, I do know that Linux does not have the extra level of
protection
that VMS provides, since it uses kseg mode, a method by which all physical
memory is mapped to a set of virtual address high up in 64-bit space.  These
addresses are only accessible from kernel mode, but it means that a wild
write
in the kernel could write into the other instance's private memory.

It all comes down to this:  We have NEVER claimed Galaxy to increase
availability.  This is not just for mixed operating systems (which, you
realize,
we don't even officially support) but for pure VMS Galaxy systems.  If you
want availability, you must have cluster nodes in separate physical machines
(and
the more you care, the more you can spent to put them on separate power
supplies,
separate rooms, separate cities, etc).  You have to look at your
availability requirements
vs your pocketbook and your other requirements and decide whether and how
Galaxy
can help you.

Burns






