> Compaq has learned the hard way that NT-on-Alpha did not fly, and that
> NT-VMS "Affinity" was not well received by the VMS base.

I'm not sure I agree with this comment.  I was quite pleased with the
Affinity concept.  The problems were two-fold.  First, MS buffaloed
then DEC into pushing a three-tiered client/server with NT client, NT
server at level two, and VMS at corporate.  This was nonsense.  In the
real world, a two-tier NT client/VMS server makes more sense, even for
fairly large companies.  Second, the integration didn't go far enough.
I'm still waiting for good integration tools for developers.  VMS 7.2
has DCOM, but this is still relatively programmer intensive, I'm not
sure about the potential for security problems, and it requires dropping
Pathworks for MacIntosh (although it may be conceivable that PW
Mac still works on 7.2, I don't know).

I guess you could also claim that it was a one-sided deal as MS should
have responded by insuring all NT products worked on alpha and
they didn't.




