> to compete with VMS, but upon products boosted by it's success.

I don't know what parallel universe you are living in but none
of the main three UNIX vendors (HP, Sun and IBM) compete
with OpenVMS. I forget the last time I saw a customer who
has made a major new OpenVMS/Alpha purchase.

This is not because these customers don't have big OpenVMS
investments they do but they arn't adding to them.

This is mainly because most of the major applications that
customers are buying do not run on OpenVMS and those that
do are being dropped. As an example the wholesale bank I
consult for are Europes largest bank and spend more on IT than
some nations GDP, they cannot buy OpenVMS based solutions
because none of their current generation of applications runs on
OpenVMS. They do have OpenVMS systems but these are
being phased out for the above reason.

What ever the attributes good or bad that OpenVMS delivers
to its customers not supporting the application that they have
chosen is a certain way of ensuring that they cannot use it.

