Damn - I just finished a quasi-apology (or at least an explanation) for all
my doom and gloom here, and here I am again with some more.  But it's hard
to pass up something that so obviously needs a reality check (sorry, Hoff):

Not having the stomach for the most superficial marketing drivel, I went
directly to the white paper.  It was still of course marketing drivel, and
only slightly less superficial, so I can't pretend to have done more than a
moderately thorough skim.  But what I found was a total of 3 references to
VMS, which was fewer than Tru64, Himalaya, or NT, but may have equaled the
number for Linux, SCO UnixWare, and NetWare.

Of those 3 references, two were simply in lists of the systems Compaq
offered.  The third was stand-alone, on page 22 - again in a list, but each
entry had at least a paragraph, so one could make some comparisons.

VMS (the last system listed) and NSK were the only Compaq OSs for which no
enhancements were indicated as planned.  For NSK it could be argued that it
might fall under the more general commitments in this section to leadership
in the Enterprise Unix market - and NSK had already been positioned
elsewhere as not only Compaq's fault-tolerant offering but it's
disaster-tolerant one as well (that should make you think a bit).  VMS was
also the only system where NT (or anything else) was offered as an
'alternative' (perhaps that sounds better than 'migration').

I don't know if the '0699' at the bottom of each page indicates the currency
of this paper, but if it does, unless the author was not up to current
Compaq views on VMS, I'd say the message is pretty clear - if you're willing
to see it.





