<david.gudewicz@abbott.com> wrote:

>spending the whole wad on getting developers back on the VMS bandwagon.  New
>apps wouldn't hurt either.  :)

I agree completely.  The best new "feature" VMS can have is simpler
licensing and a lower price.  At the moment the licensing scheme is a
nightmare for the uninitiated...such as most of the sales staff...and
the price isn't at all competitive.

*We* know that NT and Linux aren't really competitive products, but
many buyers consider them equivalent.  One is about $150 and the other
is free....VMS for a 4000-90 is over $1500, and it gets worse the more
powerful the machine you put it on...which is something else that
buyers don't understand and are annoyed by...why should it cost more
just because I run it on more expensive hardware?  Looks a lot like
price gouging those who can apparently afford it...a bad appearance
when you are trying to make a sale. 

There's also the hassle of license transfer when you sell the
hardware.  You need special forms, documentation...and $300.  To
transfer an NT license you hand over the CD, floppies and manuals to
the new buyer.  Linux is already licensed for *everyone*, so there's
no transfer in the first place. ;^)

Some of this stuff made some sort of sense back when a system cost
$250K...an extra few thousand for an OS was a small percentage of the
total, and $300 to transfer the license when you unloaded it a few
years later for 20 grand wasn't a big deal.  In today's world of $8000
systems that are sold a few years later for $500 it's just not
reasonable.

The Hobbyist program has a good chance of reviving lagging interest in
VMS, but not unless Compaq changes the business model for the OS a lot
to get it in line with today's realities.  The size of the market that
can, or will, do things the way they were done in the past is
shrinking fast.  This is not synonymous with saying that the market
for VMS is shrinking fast...in fact, it could be growing with a little
effort, intelligence and willingness to do things in new ways in
marketing and sales.

  -- Mike "make it hard to buy and people won't" Bartman --
