In article <377C00B5.48FF1DE0@tsoft-inc.com>, David A Froble <davef@tsoft-inc.com> writes:
:...I think you called this one correctly.  Trust.  Guess that's why NT/2000
:or whatever they'll call it might never find entrance to these Galaxies. :-)

  Rogue writes can certainly cause problems for other instances in an
  OpenVMS Galaxy.  These same rogue writes -- or ignoring or otherwise 
  bypassing the system configuration information provided to the operating 
  system by the console -- would likely also cause problems and have the
  potential for crashes on standalone systems as well.

  I would tend to expect a rogue write would require a several-step failure, 
  as the operating system would have to erronously map the physical memory 
  and then erroneously write to it -- each instance operates in physical 
  memory that is not normally mapped into the address space of the other 
  instances operating in the OpenVMS Galaxy.

  That said, OpenVMS Galaxy is not a fault-tolerant design, and is not 
  intended to replace a fault-tolerant system -- there are existing and 
  known cases where a hardware failure that occurs in one instance (in
  certain current AlphaServer hardware implementations) will crash a 
  different instance, or potentially crash more than one instance.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




