Bob Hassinger wrote:

> Certainly it does not make sense in general to overdesign by a factor of
> ten.  That is, to buy ten times what is currently required (although it
> can - I understand in Internet communications for example some of the
> more prudent operators are looking almost that far ahead because of the
> lag times they experience in getting new capacity vs. the growth rates
> they are seeing).
>
> The point I was making was not about overbuilding, but rather picking
> the technology to build with.  There seems to be a tendency to buy into
> technology that looks good enough at current load levels and ignoring
> what will happen when you try to scale it, leaving that for another
> day.  I suggest the best policy is to buy into a technology at the
> beginning that will scale, and which will degrade gracefully rather than
> collapse when it _is_ overloaded as will inevitably happen at some point
> along the way.  Failure to do so indicates a less that prudent design
> philosophy in a field like this where explosive, exponential growth is
> known to be a common event, and where the results can be serious
> detrimental to the business.
>

I would agree that the good enough mentality is at the root
of many systems failures and is particularly evident in the NT
space. Anyone based in the UK and watching the news about
delays at the UK passport office which are causing 100,000s
of people loads of hassle are seing in part the product of the "good
enough" school of systems design. The "Where do you want to
go today" MS slogan is probably lost on people who have had to
queue for 2 days in the rain to be able to go on holiday.

But there are degrees of good enough. It does not make much
sense to architect a system that is capable of supporting 10x
more thoughput than your marketing people are predicting.

>
> Obviously I say that here in a forum dedicated to discussion of certain
> technologies we believe to poses those characteristics to a
> significantly greater degree than the particular technologies that
> happen to have been selected for the system in question.  :-)
>

Sure but then OpenVMS was probably never an option for eBay
given the applications that they have chosen and the lack of
availability of said applications on OpenVMS.

There is also no independent benchmark data that proves OpenVMS's
scalability say supporting an Oracle OLTP database so eBay
would also have been taking a leap in the dark. If there was a
very large OpenVMS/RDB or Oracle TPC-C number for example
then OpenVMS's claims to scalability would be more credible
and as a competitor less easy to squash.

You could also argue that real world events tend to disprove
the argument that OpenVMS is in anyway immune from the
problems of scaling up to deliver increased throughput. The
eTrade problems were caused in part if I remember correctly by
an upgrade designed to increase capacity.

The lessons of most of the eCommerce systems failures from
eTrade to eBay and Schwab is that it is difficult to predict
what capacity you will require and that no architectures are
invunerable in this kind of environment. The only vendors
out there without a major eCommerce failure under their
belt are HP and thats because they don't have many large
eCommerce sites. It is also probably a good reason why
not to use HP. Sun, Compaq and IBM should have all
learnt lessons from their respective problems.

>
> Bob Hassinger
>
> Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> >
