> In addition, perhaps it would be prudent in a case like this, were it
> should be easy to anticipate high growth rates, to start by selecting
> designs and technologies that will degrade gracefully under heavy load
> rather than precipitously collapsing.
>

It is easy after the event to point out that mistakes have been made
in the design of a system due to incorrect predictions about the growth
in traffic to that system.

eBays transaction growth was apparently an order of magnitude more
than they expected.

The problem for companies like eBay and other eCommerce sites who
have suffered simular growing pains is that in practice it is very hard
to predict this.

When one implements something like an ERP system one has a good
idea about things like the user population and projected growth in
user population,  one is dealing with a finite number employees and
a for example a budgeted increase in head count. Even within this much
more controlled environment mistakes are often made in predicting
the capacity required for an ERP system. 25 % of Sun's sales into
the SAP market come from what SAP call roll-overs. These are where
the origional platfrom chosen to support SAP did not do the job, normally
because it could not provide the actual capacity. Many of these systems were
origionally NT boxes but a significant number coem off HP or IBM SP2's.

NT is the most popular platform for SAP in terms of the number of servers
installed for SAP (though not users) but it also has the highest failure
rate.

eBay and other sites like them are driven by the market and this because
of the nature of the internet is difficult to predict. In the case of eBay they
also had no historical data to use as a reference for their sizings.

eBay could of course have said lets be very safe and build an architecture
that is designed to accomodate 10x more capacity than they expected. But
this has potential financial drawbacks if the 10x or 5x growth does not
happen and could have left eBay with over engineered systems.

regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect






