Certainly it does not make sense in general to overdesign by a factor of
ten.  That is, to buy ten times what is currently required (although it
can - I understand in Internet communications for example some of the
more prudent operators are looking almost that far ahead because of the
lag times they experience in getting new capacity vs. the growth rates
they are seeing).

The point I was making was not about overbuilding, but rather picking
the technology to build with.  There seems to be a tendency to buy into
technology that looks good enough at current load levels and ignoring
what will happen when you try to scale it, leaving that for another
day.  I suggest the best policy is to buy into a technology at the
beginning that will scale, and which will degrade gracefully rather than
collapse when it _is_ overloaded as will inevitably happen at some point
along the way.  Failure to do so indicates a less that prudent design
philosophy in a field like this where explosive, exponential growth is
known to be a common event, and where the results can be serious
detrimental to the business.

Obviously I say that here in a forum dedicated to discussion of certain
technologies we believe to poses those characteristics to a
significantly greater degree than the particular technologies that
happen to have been selected for the system in question.  :-)

Bob Hassinger


