In article <v7Ce3.50$Ct4.354@ozemail.com.au>, Laurence Blake
<URL:mailto:laurence@as.com.au> wrote:
> Hi
> 
> I've got a Alpha XL 300 with 64 MB RAM.
> It has four 16 MB modules in 4 of the slots.
> 
> I wanted to go to 128 MB so Digital's agent sent me two 32 MB modules.
> I put them in and PC just beeps and beeps with no boot.
> Take them out and all is ok.
> 
> Do I have to fill all the slots?
> Do I have to make a motherboard change?
> What is the correct way to upgrade to 128 from 64
> 
> Can I use RAM from an Intel based PC?
> 
> I don't have a manual so can anyone please point me in the right direction

The Celebris Alpha XL 300 needs 4 SIMMs of each. There are eight slots
so two groups are possible. Specification: 72 pins, 36 bit (true
parity), 70 ns.   AFAIK there are even problems when faster memory is
used.  You need an extra pair of 32 MB SIMMs of the same specification
if they did send you the right specification otherwise you need 4 of
them. A total of 512 MB is possible (8 x 64). 

There are Intel based PCs that have the same SIMMs like the DEC Celebris
Pentium range. But most PCs don't have the 36 bit type.

Ernst
